GXEMUL-SEC\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_093043\codex.err:239:.\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_033247\codex.err:5459:tools/pipeline/matrix.html:3984:construction rather than by an oracle.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — *** THIS ITEM NOW ARRIVES WITH ITS ROWS ALREADY BUILT AND MEASURED, which changes it from a hunt into an implementation. ***  E2 (`|=` -&gt; `=` on a latch) and E3 (widened guard) are STATEMENT defects, not constant-expression ones -- so unlike E1 they cannot be closed by a declaration, and the seat gave the reason: no constant expression can observe a statement, so the by-construction route is exhausted at E1.  A latch HELPER MACRO does not close E2 either: a macro-level `=` still escapes all 20 rows, because every row exercises one latch in isolation.  MEASURED against the ask-again&#x27;s surviving binaries, one frozen instrument: base 22/22 (negative control -- neither row manufactures a failure), `E2_assignA` 21/22 with `latch keep` moving (got 2, want 1), `E3_widenA` 21/22 with the two-site row moving (got 0,1 want 1,1).  Both had previously escaped at 20/20, and the two are ORTHOGONAL -- each is green under the other&#x27;s mutant.  `latch keep` = TOC_SHORT + BAD_FMT + TOC_SHORT, counting the #489 TOC diagnostic; its channel is a latch OTHER THAN the one Site A owns, which is exactly why all 20 shipped rows were blind.  The two-site row = BAD_CMD + BAD_FMT: both existing two-site rows fix FMT-then-CMD, where `warned` is 0 at Site A, so a widened guard is indistinguishable -- ONE REVERSED SESSION covers the whole order-asymmetry class.  *** RENAME BEFORE SHIPPING: the seat&#x27;s own name `both sites rev` is exactly 14 chars = the gate&#x27;s column width, so it renders with a trailing space and the gate anchor would miss it.  Use `rev sites`.  The seat found this by noticing its own summary table printed blank for that row while the FAIL list caught it. ***  NOT added to #490: E2/E3 were adjudicated coverage debt rather than floor breaches, and 20 -&gt; 22 rows moves every score in a 13-build table.  More witnesses create more candidate ROUNDS, never bigger rounds. — evidence: [panel_20260829_133206]  measured, supp_p2_20260829/probe_{base,E2_assignA,E3_widenA}.log</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</