_re_width_probe.py&#x27;s `other kill` row is a DEATH-ONLY oracle -- a one-character change to the text it claims to pin scores 35/35, MEASURED</span><span class="tip">sgi_re_width_probe.py&#x27;s `other kill` row is a DEATH-ONLY oracle -- a one-character change to the text it claims to pin scores 35/35, MEASURED — witness: harness:MEASURED by the #497 pass-2 seat in its own clone, with the prediction filed BEFORE building.  base 35/35 CONTROL=OK; x_othertext (catch-all message text altered, exit(1) untouched) 35/35 with ZERO red rows; x_readtext (one read arm&#x27;s text altered) 34/35, reddening `tlb_a ld kill got=exit1-txt want=exit1+txt`.  The matched pair is the point: it proves the six siblings ARE failable and this one is not.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">holdphase</span><span class="ttl">the hold-marker discriminator was implemented with `&gt;=` where the carrier says LATER, and six holds were cleared by SAME-DATE siblings written by the batch that created the marker</span><span class="tip">the hold-marker discriminator was implemented with `&gt;=` where the carrier says LATER, and six holds were cleared by SAME-DATE siblings written by the batch that created the marker — witness: harness:MEASURED by the codex and opus5 seats on the #497b pass-2 panel, independently, and confirmed here row by row before reverting.  SIX markers were wrongly cleared, ALL of them phase=regress / seat=fable5: ns16550, carrier, es438, sh4sci, wdcdataoutdrain, and wdccdbmidxfer entry [26].  *** THIS FIELD PREVIOUSLY NAMED THE SIXTH AS &#x27;research/opus5, answered at review&#x27;.  THAT IS THE MARKER THE COMMIT **KEPT** -- disposition (b) -- NOT the one it cleared.  Caught by the fable5 seat; I had copied the framing from another seat&#x27;s table without checking which entry it pointed at. ***  widthkillclass was the one CORRECT clearance: held at review, all seven named seats answered at review.  A strictly-later + same-stage test keeps all six and clears that one.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">gxprobepccache</span><span class="ttl">gxprobe cached the pc across `step`, so a driver that stepped without _exec_at() had its next pc assignment silently skipped -- a detector row that could not fail</span><span class="tip">gxprobe cached the pc across `step`, so a driver that stepped without _exec_at() had its next pc assignment silently skipped -- a detector row that could not fail — witness: harness:MEASURED by the #497-family measuring seat and re-verified here.  `gxprobe.py` skips a redundant register assignment when `cache_regs` is on; `_exec_at()` pops the cached pc after stepping, but a driver calling `send(&quot;step&quot;)` DIRECTLY did not -- so its next `set_reg(&quot;pc&quot;, a)` was dropped as redundant.  The transcript is unambiguous: the first access carries a `pc=` line, the second and third do not, and they execute `nop`.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">sgideprimop</span><span class="ttl">dev_sgi_re.c draw_primitive(): a guest-written drawing op ends the host -- 254 of 256 top-byte values reach `default: fatal(); exit(1);`</span><span class="tip">dev_sgi_re.c draw_primitive(): a guest-written drawing op ends the host -- 254 of 256 top-byte values reach `default: fatal(); exit(1);` — witness: probe:regress/artifacts/sgideprimop/witness_20260831.sh -- rung-3 on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32`.  DE_PRIM_TRIANGLE (0x02000000) and DE_PRIM_POINT (0x00000000) each kill the host with the op echoed in the diagnostic; DE_PRIM_RECTANGLE (0x03000000) survives with zero kill messages.  The RECTANGLE control is what makes it discriminating rather than merely fatal.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — ADJUDICATION of nine dispositions, verified by opening files rather than by reasoning; it stated its line numbers matched the brief&#x27;s ranges, proving it was in the fork and not a pristine copy.  **Zero dispositions reversed on substance; two corrected.**  *** ITS CENTRAL FINDING PREVENTED THE ROUND FROM MINTING THE DEFECT IT HAD JUST FILED. ***  The natural oracle-correction for `tail_acc_x` is `exit1` -&gt; `exit0` (*the host now survives*).  Traced as a CHANNEL rather than an intent: under a future one-token death reinstatement -- `exit(0)` at the default arm -- the session announces once and dies, `how` is `exit0`, the re-pinned row reads GREEN, `tail_acc` still counts 1, no other session reaches `draw_primitive()`, and the detector scores a **TOTAL ESCAPE, 29/29**.  **Exit status cannot vary between *declined and quit* and *died with status 0*.**  Repair: re-pin onto a positive post-draw observable (a load through the device; `printed()` already reads `died`), keeping an exit-status row only as a second channel.  **L1 -- a row under construction -- so no stopping-rule tension**, and the round owes a FOURTH mutant: death-by-`exit(0)` at the default arm.  *Knowable at writing time from the CHANNEL check, which is exactly why that check runs before the table.*  **Corrected my `exit0oracle` census: 13 sites, not 9** -- my grep anchored on the variable name `how` and missed four rows using `how1`..`how4`.  **Upheld the RANKING with a measurement I did not have**: it built a decline-then-`exit(0)` mutant of `#498` and found `lo_sent` KILLS it via the read-side sentinel even though all four `*_alive` rows pass -- so the blindness is per-ROW, not per-detector, and no shipped detector is floor-breached as a whole.  **Re-verified F5 mechanically rather than accepting my ruling in my own favour**: `DRAW` is a PREFIX of the fatal and `(once)` appends after it, and the `tail_acc` session is a fresh process issuing exactly one draw-triggering access -- so first occurrence = only occurrence = 1, latched or not.  Keep-the-text confirmed, and it noted the current text stays TRUE post-fix (still unimplemented, now declined), so there is no wrong-record pressure to reword.  **One records correction against me on F1:** `#498`&#x27;s own probe header ALREADY carried the reset-value fact in its DOES-NOT-CATCH section.  What is new is the headline promotion and the `memset` verification a packet-fed seat&#x27;s refusal forced -- *the round should credit the header rather than book a fresh discovery*.  Also flagged two records nits, both correct: my &quot;one uncommitted edit&quot; undercounted, and `CHECKPOINT.md`&#x27;s HEAD had gone stale by its own rule. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — THE NINTH SEAT; this stage was held open for it.  **Q1: the precedent is not elsewhere in the tree -- it is INSIDE `draw_primitive()` itself, twice.**  The ROP `default:` arms latch-and-announce without exiting, and the XFER guard in the same function has its `exit(1)` COMMENTED OUT.  So decline + latched announce is the file&#x27;s own idiom and the fix is not an import.  It killed the other three members on facts: ACCEPT needs a computed-then-discarded value and nothing computes a POINT or TRIANGLE; FAULT is an ADDRESS-SPACE disposition and this is a command-register VALUE, with no space to leave unmapped; BACKED ACCEPT fabricates storage where there is no register.  *** Q2 -- A FIFTH MUTANT, AND IT IS A FLOOR OBLIGATION RATHER THAN COVERAGE DEBT. ***  **`M5 primdrop`: delete the `case DE_PRIM_RECTANGLE:` LABEL** so RECTANGLE falls into the de-fatalised `default:`.  One line, and plausible rather than contrived because the four documented primitives INTERLEAVE.  Not covered by M1-M4: guard intact, bit intact, `|=` intact, and NO DEATH at all -- strictly quieter than M4.  **Measured, not argued: `grep` for `PRIMITIVE|0x2060|0x01000000|0x03000000` across all three shipped probes returns ZERO hits -- not one probe in the tree programs the primitive register or issues a legal LINE or RECTANGLE**, so every row I prescribed tests only the DECLINE side and the mutant sweeps all three clean.  **Why it is FLOOR and not debt:** the de-escalation clause requires the detector shipping WITH the fix to pin the ACCEPT SIDE of each predicate the guard gated -- *part of the fix&#x27;s cost*.  **My PRETABLE section 2 enumerated five rows and not one is an accept row.**  Before the fix, deleting that label meant `exit(1)` on the guest&#x27;s first rectangle -- unmissable; after it, one `(once)` line and a console that never renders text, since the file records at `:760-761` that OpenBSD draws characters this way.  **That is the accidental tripwire the fix destroys.**  Kill channel, needing no framebuffer read-back: a legal RECTANGLE with `DE_DRAWMODE_ROP` set and an unimplemented `rop`, asserting `count(DRAW) == 0` AND `count(&quot;[ sgi_de: RECT: rop[&quot;) == 1` -- an ARM-UNIQUE anchor (verified: two hits in the file, LINE and RECT).  Caveat that decides the session shape: `rop_used` is function-static, so the anchor fires once per PROCESS.  *** AND A HAZARD IN THE VERY PRECEDENT THAT LICENSES THE FIX: COPY THE SHAPE, NOT THE SCOPE. ***  `static char rop_used[256]` and `static int first` are FUNCTION-STATIC -- process-global, shared by every device instance, the `n_trunc_msgs` anti-pattern.  Census: **4 lines, ALL in this file, ALL inside `draw_primitive()`** -- the tree&#x27;s only such sites.  `d-&gt;warned` + `(once)` is the house form.  It also recorded a tension rather than resolving it: the in-file precedent latches PER VALUE while this fix latches ONE BIT for 254 op classes, so POINT-then-TRIANGLE prints once.  Deliberate (it is M1&#x27;s killer) but **the round must say why it did not use an `(op &gt;&gt; 24)`-indexed table**, or a reader takes the difference for an oversight.  *Hygiene: it reported that the tree was committed under it twice mid-review, checked that none of the five files it cites changed, and confirmed it wrote nothing.* — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING.  **CORRECTED HALF OF MY OWN HEADLINE CLAIM.**  I recorded POINT as what *an uninitialised OR PARTIALLY-WRITTEN op register* produces.  The reset half is proven (`memset(d, 0, sizeof(struct sgi_re_data))` zeroes `de_reg[]`); **the partially-written half is FALSE** -- `#498`&#x27;s width guard declines byte and halfword accesses BEFORE `de_reg[]` is updated, so accepted 32/64-bit writes always store complete words.  *One of this round&#x27;s own fixes closed the door I was citing.*  **DID THE OPTION-SET PRECEDENT GREP the three-membered-set rule demands**, and the tree answers clearly: `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c` declines an unimplemented command with a per-instance `(once)` diagnostic and completes normally, `dev_m8820x.c` ignores one; `dev_asc.c` raises a guest interrupt ONLY because it has an explicitly modelled illegal-command channel, which this site does not.  That is why FAULT is wrong here -- an in-tree warrant, not an argument.  *** TWO MORE ONE-CHARACTER MUTANT CLASSES, ONE OF WHICH IS NEW TO THIS PROJECT. ***  (a) `1 &lt;&lt; 7` -&gt; `1 &lt;&lt; 6` aliases an existing bit -- the `#498` collision class, killed by pre-arming all seven existing latch bits in one session before triggering.  (b) **`d-&gt;warned |= SGIRE_W_PRIMOP` -&gt; `d-&gt;warned = SGIRE_W_PRIMOP`, one character, which CLEARS EVERY EARLIER LATCH** -- killed by an A -&gt; primitive -&gt; A session requiring A&#x27;s message exactly once.  It proposes combining them: fire all seven latch sites, trigger POINT, revisit the sites, and require every message exactly once.  *** AND A VACUITY FINDING AGAINST SHIPPED MATERIAL: `exit0` IS NOT A SAFE SURVIVAL ORACLE. ***  *&quot;A wrong `exit(0)` still kills the emulator and satisfies that oracle.&quot;*  `#498`&#x27;s `hi_alive`/`lo_alive`/`lo_alive2` rows all pin `how == &quot;exit0&quot;`.  Assert the `alive` value captured BEFORE the harness sends `quit`, ideally with a post-trigger command.  Filed as its own row -- it is coverage debt against a shipped detector above the floor, not an L2.  **GUEST-CONTROL TRACE, concrete:** RE base `0x15000000`, DE mapping `0x15002000`, `CRIME_DE_PRIMITIVE` offset `0x60`, START alias `0x60 | 0x800 = 0x860`, so a guest `sw` to **`0xffffffffb5002860`** with `t0 = 0` draws a POINT.  `startFlag` captures bit `0x800` and is cleared; `regnr = 0x60/4`.  **And `if (startFlag)` is NOT conditioned on `MEM_WRITE`, so a START-aliased READ also invokes drawing** -- worth a row of its own.  Notes that `break`-inside-latch is a non-mutant here: `default` is last and the function has no tail, so moving or deleting that `break` changes nothing. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — UNKNOWN — *** RESERVED FOR MEASUREMENT by owner ruling.  NOT a seat failure and NOT agreement. *** — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING.  Checked the tree rather than the packet and said so: noticed HEAD was two commits past the brief&#x27;s `e898c84`, read the queue row, and confirmed **nothing has shipped for this row** -- the same premise error the measuring seat caught on the sibling round.  Independently confirmed the mask/constant table, the no-common-tail claim (*&quot;the switch&#x27;s closing brace is the last statement&quot;*), that `op` is guest-controlled through `CRIME_DE_PRIMITIVE` and *is already DE_PRIM_POINT at RESET with no store*, and that bits [31:26] are unused by every `DE_PRIM_` constant so `DE_PRIM_LINE | 0x04000000` dies.  Chose **decline + latched announce**, not FAULT, and ruled the caller should NOT see the decline.  *** ITS BEST FINDING IS A THIRD BLAST-RADIUS TIER THAT BOTH I AND THE PACKET-FED SEATS MISSED. ***  I had corrected the radius to *one certain row (tail_acc_x) and one conditional on wording (tail_acc)*.  grok added: **a REWORD does not RED `hi_draw`/`hi_rd_draw` -- it BLINDS them.**  Both assert `count(DRAW) == 0`, so once the substring no longer exists they stay green *for the wrong reason* -- vacuous by construction, invisible to every gate.  It also caught that my `gate_arm.sh` comment said *both rows* red, *&quot;That comment overstates the same way the brief does&quot;* -- an L4 in shipped material, corrected in place.  **The design constraint that follows is sharper than mine: KEEP THE MESSAGE TEXT and add `(once)`.**  Keeping it costs ONE re-pin; rewording costs two re-pins AND silently blinds two further rows.  Disclosed honestly that it did not re-run the probe -- *&quot;this is a read of the live oracles against the code paths they exercise&quot;* -- which is the READING/MEASUREMENT distinction stated without being asked. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING; went to the tree for everything it could not settle and said which.  *** CAUGHT A REAL DEFECT IN MY BRIEF: I INLINED THE SUPERSEDED LATCH CENSUS. ***  My settled-premises block gave `(once)` 9, `(further occurrences suppressed)` 3, `(reported once)` 2.  **Those are the 2026-08-26 literals that the carrier itself DEMOTES in writing** -- *&quot;Superseded literals, kept only as the demoted record ... Never a current claim&quot;* -- re-derived on 08-30 as 24/4/5.  kimi measured 25/4/5 and I re-ran the carrier&#x27;s own deriver: **25 / 4 / 5**, confirming kimi and refuting my brief.  *The carrier names a deriver precisely so nobody transcribes the number, and I transcribed the one it had crossed out.*  The count grew 24 -&gt; 25 because THIS session&#x27;s `#498` added a `(once)` site -- the rounds that consult the number are the rounds that change it, which is the carrier&#x27;s stated reason for the deriver.  **Its conclusion is unaffected and strengthened: `(once)` is not merely the plurality but the overwhelming majority.**  *** ITS SECOND CORRECTION IS WRONG, AND CHECKING IT VINDICATED THE CARRIER. ***  kimi reports that the tree&#x27;s counter-style outlier *&quot;lives in `src/core/memory.c`&quot;* and that it saw **no counter** there, only a verbosity gate with a probe-tolerance early-out.  Measured: `src/cpus/memory_rw.c` DOES carry `static int n_trunc_msgs = 0;` with `n_trunc_msgs &lt; 8` and `n_trunc_msgs ++` -- a real counter, exactly as the carrier describes.  **kimi found a DIFFERENT diagnostic** -- `memory_warn_about_unimplemented_addr()` in `core/memory.c`, which `memory_rw.c` calls and which genuinely has no counter.  Two similar-looking diagnostics in one area; it searched for the described behaviour and landed on the wrong one.  *Recorded rather than quietly dropped: a seat&#x27;s correction carries authority, and this one would have sent a future round to rewrite an accurate carrier paragraph.*  *** AND A THIRD Q4 OBSERVER THAT grok AND codex BOTH MISSED: THE HARDCODED ROW DENOMINATOR. ***  `regress/gate_arm.sh` checks `sgi_de rows green` against a literal `29`.  When `tail_acc_x` reds, that count falls to 28 and **the denominator check reds too** -- so the oracle-correction re-pin must bump the literal in the same edit, or the round meets two reds and attributes one of them wrongly.  Neither other file-reading seat named it. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED.  *** THE ROUND&#x27;S BEST FINDING AND IT LANDS AGAINST THE AUTHOR: the 2-of-4 reframing is a PARTITION of the 254/256 claim, not a correction, and recording only the sharper half is the locally-true restatement that hides the defect. ***  Also asked the one checkable caveat nobody else did -- are op bits [31:26] live? -- which on checking WIDENED the defect: the primitive field is bits [25:24] and the mask is [31:24], so a legal LINE or RECTANGLE with a stray high bit also dies.  Named four premises as not settleable from the packet, each with the single fact that would settle it, and separated the all-zeroes CONSTANT from the register&#x27;s RESET VALUE. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED.  Supplied the clause-(d) mutant BEFORE the fix exists -- drop the `!` from the guard, so the latch never sets and the announce never fires while the bit name and the absent exit(1) both still read correct -- AND the row that kills it: two distinct unimplemented primitives in one session, expecting exactly one message.  Precision correction: 254/256 counts MASKED TOP-BYTE CLASSES, not full 32-bit op values. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — PACKET-FED.  Refused the no-common-tail premise and answered CONDITIONALLY ON BOTH BRANCHES, naming #498&#x27;s `goto`-to-reach-the-tail as the governing precedent if a tail exists.  Marked the uninitialised-register inference as REASONED rather than READ -- the distinction the author had blurred. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_033247</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">pcc2widthkills</span><span class="ttl">dev_pcc2.c: TEN live width kills (of twelve guards -- #119 already fixed two in this same file, and its shape is the answer)</span><span class="tip">dev_pcc2.c: TEN live width kills (of twelve guards -- #119 already fixed two in this same file, and its shape is the answer) — witness: probe:regress/artifacts/pcc2widthkills/witness_20260831.sh -- a RUNG-3 witness. Real m88k guest instructions through real decode on unmodified `-E mvme88k -e mvme187`: the trace shows `st r1,r2,0x0 ; [0xfff4201c] = 0x00000042` followed by `TODO: pcc2: non-byte reads and writes of PCCTWO_SCCERR`.  Built by the kimi seat, REPRODUCED independently by the coordinator with a `put b` negative control and a `put w` positive control.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — Reached the 5-of-10 split independently of kimi, from the alignment rule plus the fact that `dev_pcc2_access` switches on the transaction&#x27;s STARTING offset.  **Refuted my settled premise 5**: a `memcpy` runs BEFORE the switch on the read path, so these are not value-declines at all -- in-range wide reads return the register span and end-crossing reads are zero-filled. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_054552</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING, and it settled the question that decides whether this row is a defect or documentation -- **against me, and in the direction that makes the row WORSE**.  *** Q1: WIDE ACCESS IS REACHABLE.  My premise was that these are byte-wide control registers and the CPU or bus glue might be unable to deliver a 2- or 4-byte access at all.  Refuted twice over, both verified here. ***  **(a) The tree&#x27;s OWN header documents the legality of what is being killed.**  `src/include/thirdparty/mvme_pcctworeg.h:9-10`: *&quot;PCCchip2 control and status register can be accessed as bytes (8 bits), two-bytes (16 bits), or four-bytes (32 bits).&quot;*  So **byte-wide is a property of this MODEL&#x27;s `switch (relative_addr)`, not of the chip the header describes** -- the guards kill on accesses the hardware documentation in this very tree says are legal.  **(b) The CPU delivers them.**  `src/cpus/cpu_m88k_instr_loadstore.c` is compiled for `LS_SIZE` 1, 2, 4 and 8, declares `uint8_t data[LS_SIZE]` and passes that size to `cpu-&gt;memory_rw`, faulting on misalignment rather than narrowing the access.  So `len` in {1,2,4,8} reaches the device.  *This is the third time tonight a seat has refuted a premise of mine, and the second where the refutation RAISED severity rather than lowering it.*  **Q2: DO NOT convert `#119`&#x27;s two width latches in this round** -- that belongs to `staticlatchscope`.  A clean scoping answer to a question I had genuinely not decided.  **Q3: A RUNG-3 PROBE IS POSSIBLE, with an address**: the debugger&#x27;s `put h` / `put w` reaches `dev_pcc2_access` at `0xfff42000 + off`.  That discharges the attempt this row owes with a concrete invocation rather than a maybe.  **Q4: the smallest survivors of a *host-lived + message-appeared* detector are a SHARED LATCH BIT across the twelve arms, and WARN-THEN-FALL-THROUGH** -- i.e. the detector must pin per-arm identity and the refusal&#x27;s EFFECT, not merely that something was announced. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_054552</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — *** STOPPED READING AND MEASURED.  Killed the host twice on the unmodified tree -- once by direct `put w`, once through REAL GUEST DECODE (hand-assembled m88k words, pc=0x1000, step 4), with the trace showing `st r1,r2,0x0 ; [0xfff4201c] = 0x00000042` then the SCCERR kill.  Reproduced independently by the coordinator. ***  Also found the probe-authorship trap that outlives this row -- **`put b` never reaches the device** (0 messages vs 1 for `put w`), because the debugger&#x27;s byte path passes NO_EXCEPTIONS and `memory_rw` skips the device call for DM_DEFAULT devices -- and measured that a wide read from an UNGUARDED base spanning guarded registers is ALREADY ACCEPTED today. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_054552</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — *** THE PANEL&#x27;S BEST ANSWER AND ITS THREE CONCLUSIONS SPLIT THREE WAYS, which is the argument for auditing premises rather than only derivations. ***  (a) Refused my unshown claim that `fatal()` returns and named its body as the settling fact -- CHECKED, it returns, so my premise was right and unsupported.  (b) Challenged `grep -c &#x27;^ *exit(1);&#x27;` as blind to other spellings -- the count held at 13, **but the four extra `exit(` hits are `#119`&#x27;s own comments, which is how the fourfold in-file precedent was found at all**.  (c) REFUTED my Q3 premise outright: I claimed the read path assigns `odata`; it appears twice in the file and both are in a DIFFERENT access function, so a declined read returns the caller&#x27;s pre-set value and the ACCEPT option&#x27;s premise is false here. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_054552</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — Ended on the point that matters for the detector: a shared message format across twelve arms makes it easy to keep the message while changing the behaviour -- i.e. rows that check only *a message appeared* are the ones a mutant will walk through. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_054552</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — Landed on the same conclusion I reached from the source: the in-file precedent&#x27;s shape is right and its latching is not.  Flagged the unlatched announce as a defect of the precedent rather than a feature to copy. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_054552</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">lcabadval</span><span class="ttl">dev_lca.c: FOUR chipset registers kill the host on a value outside a small accepted set -- the #443 BADVAL class</span><span class="tip">dev_lca.c: FOUR chipset registers kill the host on a value outside a small accepted set -- the #443 BADVAL class — witness: harness:ENUMERATED from the shipped source, 2026-08-31.  Eleven live terminating calls.  The value guards, each ending in exit(1): `idata &amp; ~1` (:206), `idata &amp; ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN` (:226), `idata != 0ULL &amp;&amp; idata != 0x300800000ULL` (:241), `idata != 0x700000ULL` (:256), `idata != 0x240000000ULL` (:280), `idata != 0x3ff00000ULL` (:295).  Each diagnostic says so in its own words -- *&quot;(0x%&quot; PRIx64&quot;) from the only implemented value&quot;*.  Plus two range guards that also kill: `dev &gt;= 21` (:129) and `ofs &gt; len` (:163).  NO rung-3 witness: READ, not booted.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — UNKNOWN — [HELD AWAITING SEAT: codex, grok, kimi, opus5, fable5]  Three packet-fed seats answered and settled what a packet can settle -- the accept-set census, and (once I checked it) which fields are consumed.  The rest needs the tree. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_053346</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — *** THE ROUND&#x27;S BEST ANSWER.  Refuted the row&#x27;s own title without needing the one fact it could not settle, and caught my brief contradicting itself between Q2 and Q3 -- do these fields feed decode or does the emulator not model them?  Both were asserted. ***  The source settles it against me: base/mask/tlb_enable are written and read back and nothing else; only `ioc_conf` is consumed (`bus = d-&gt;ioc_conf &amp; 1`).  **That splits the fix design** -- three arms are pure storage where a decline may keep the store, and the fourth is not.  Also the sharpest design point of the panel: for `W_BASE0` the accepted `0` is the MAXIMALLY ORDINARY write, so an accept row pinned to the exotic magic constant misfires on the most legal write rather than an unusual one.  Named `IOC_TB_ENA_TEN` as the single fact that would close the census, which is exactly the right move for a packet-fed seat. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_053346</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — Derived the accept sets independently and reached the same correction.  Held the line on reachability: named an LCA IOC register specification or PROM source as the one thing that would settle whether these are hardware-legal values or merely the ones this emulator was developed against.  There is no such document in `_scratchpad/`. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_053346</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — Went arm by arm and attached a single named settling fact to each rather than answering around the gaps -- the discipline this project asks of a packet-fed seat. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_053346</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — UNKNOWN — [HELD AWAITING SEAT: fable5]  The rung-3 seat says a witness IS constructible and specified a full sequence, and it refuted three of this row&#x27;s own records (six value-guarded arms not four; eleven live exit(1) not twelve; three arms of six accept exactly one value, not one of four -- so the ORIGINAL title was closer to right than the correction that overturned it).  It also named a CONFOUND that would make the probe lie: lca_interrupt_assert and lca_interrupt_deassert are bare fatal()+exit(1) with the whole ISA PIC chain wired to them, so ANY interrupt ends the host with the SAME status as the value guards under test.  THE QUESTION FOR THE FLAGSHIP SEAT: given that every row must therefore match the fatal() TEXT rather than the exit status, and given that lcaconfwidth (filed today) means the IOC_CONF arm&#x27;s text can PRINT THE WRONG VALUE, is the text-matching oracle sound at THAT arm -- or must lcaconfwidth&#x27;s printf fix land FIRST, making it a prerequisite of this row rather than a sibling?  Second: the de-escalation clause owes an accept row PER PREDICATE, and the ledger&#x27;s earlier census dropped W_BASE1 and W_MASK1 -- confirm the accept-row set is six, not four. — evidence: [HELD AWAITING SEAT: fable5]</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — MANUAL — RUNG-3 ATTEMPT: MADE 2026-08-31 per the owner ruling, by a reading seat.  *** RESULT: A WITNESS IS CONSTRUCTIBLE AND A FULL SEQUENCE IS SPECIFIED -- so this row is NOT documented-only, and `none&#x27; would have meant `did not think to ask&#x27;. ***  Target `-E alpha -e alphabook1 0x4000:/dev/null&#x27;; machine_alpha.c&#x27;s ST_ALPHABOOK1 is the ONLY subtype calling device_add(machine,&quot;lca&quot;).  Construction and debugger-prompt arrival are ALREADY MEASURED elsewhere in this repo -- CHANGELOG #279&#x27;s blast-radius gate lists alphabook1 among the twelve machines reaching the prompt, and gate_asan_sweep.sh&#x27;s AB_EXEMPT lists alpha/3000/300 and NOT alphabook1.  Guest VA is K0SEG (paddr | 0xfffffc0000000000, memory_alpha.c), with the ptbr==0 identity path as a second route.  A guest `stq a1,0(a0)&#x27; = 0xB6300000 reaches DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc) via LS_GENERIC_N -&gt; memory_rw.
GXEMUL-SEC\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_093043\codex.err:1374: 13407: ### `lcabadval` (HIGH) -- four registers accept exactly one value each and kill on the rest
GXEMUL-SEC\_scratchpad\panel_20260831_093043\codex.err:1378: 13411:         fatal("... (0x%" PRIx64") from the only implemented value
GXEMUL-SEC\tools\pipeline\ledger.json:24374:   "state_note": "OPEN, and the committed artifact in `witness` is the REPRODUCTION, not the fix.  `regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py` shipped with #497 and carries SIX `ld kill` rows that assert THE HOST STILL DIES on an 8-byte access at each of the six windows -- they pin the defect, they do not repair it.  #497 de-fatalised the WIDTH guard only; the width test is evaluated FIRST, so a legal `ld` still reaches the kill.  *** THE ORDERING OBLIGATION IS ALREADY RECORDED IN #497's ROUND BLOCK AND IS THE REASON THIS ROW MUST NOT BE CLOSED BY THE CHECKER: fixing sgirekills will turn a GREEN GATE RED BY SUCCEEDING, six rows' worth. ***  It therefore inherits the oracle-correction ordering -- re-pin the six rows FIRST, measure the committed build RED under the new expectations, THEN edit the code.  A row whose own detector must invert is exactly the case the shipped-work heuristic cannot read.\n\n*** COUNT CHECK 2026-09-06, AND MY FIRST CORRECTION WAS ALSO WRONG.  THE LIVE COUNT IS TWELVE. ***\n\nThree numbers have now been attached to this row: the title says TWENTY-ONE, my correction an hour ago said\nNINETEEN, and the live count is **TWELVE**.\n\n    grep -c 'exit(1)'                    19   <- ALL occurrences, comments included\n    grep -cE '//[[:space:]]*exit\\(1\\)'    4   <- commented-out kills (:218, :227, :689, :1251)\n    (three more sit inside comment PROSE at :321, :325, :945, discussing exit(1))\n    grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);'    12   <- LIVE, statement position\n\n*** THE INSTRUCTIVE PART IS WHY I BELIEVED 19, BECAUSE IT IS A NEW COROLLARY OF A RULE THIS PROJECT ALREADY\nHAS. ***  I wrote that `grep -c 'exit('` and `grep -c 'exit(1)'` BOTH returned 19, and treated the agreement\nas ruling out the very error that had just made `lcabadval`'s enumeration wrong -- a commented-out kill\ncounted as live.  **Both greps share the same blindness: neither excludes `//`.**  The carrier says when two\nderivers DISAGREE neither is trustworthy until you look at what each matched.  The corollary, learned here:\n**when two derivers AGREE that is not evidence either, if they can fail the same way.**  Agreement between\nmethods is worth exactly the independence of the methods -- which is the unanimity-on-a-brief-supplied-fact\nrule, one level down and pointed at greps instead of seats.\n\nAnd it is the third instance tonight of *a correction is not safer than the thing it corrects*: `#503`'s\npass-3 refutation displaced a truer sentence, `83df95c` sharpened a vague claim while reading as though it\nfixed a false one, and this correction replaced a wrong number with another wrong number.\n\n**THE TITLE IS LEFT AS WRITTEN AND IS NOT SILENTLY RE-GRADED**: 21 may have been right when the row was\nopened, with sites removed since -- four of the nineteen are commented out, which is consistent with someone\nhaving disabled them.  A round on this file DERIVES the count from the statement-position pattern above\nbefore acting, and does not trust any of the three numbers in this row.\n\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP RECORD 2026-09-07 (triage stage) -- written into state_note, NOT into `entries'. ***\n`entries' is the SEAT ANSWER list: check_stage_panels.py counts entries to decide whether a stage's\nnine seats were fired, and gen_dashboard.py REFUSES TO RENDER on a seat id no column declares.  A\nmain-loop note parked there is not merely mis-filed -- it MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER\nHAPPENED, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  Seven such entries were\nwritten during this session and all seven were migrated here when the generator refused.\n\nL4 -- TITLE CORRECTED.  The state_note has carried the derived count of **TWELVE** since 2026-09-06, and the\nTITLE still said TWENTY-ONE.  **A note that corrects a title does not correct the title**, and the title is\nwhat every queue listing, dashboard row and triage pass shows first -- so the corrected figure was sitting\none field away from the wrong one, losing every time.\n\nOld title, kept because the quote-what-you-refute rule applies to my own records too:\n  \"dev_sgi_re.c carries 21 live terminating calls -- 15 of them inside a DEVICE_ACCESS and 13 in DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re) alone -- and the six READ arms are all guest-reachable\"\n\nRe-derived at HEAD before this edit: `grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);' src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c' = **12**,\nagainst 19 raw hits.  The new title NAMES ITS DERIVER, per the counts rule, so the next reader re-runs it\ninstead of trusting it -- which is the whole remedy for a class that has now had three generations on this\none row.\n\n**The DEVICE_ACCESS half of the old title (`15 of them inside a DEVICE_ACCESS ... 13 in DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re)\nalone') IS DELIBERATELY NOT REPLACED WITH A NEW NUMBER**, because [[exitcensus]] is open against exactly that\nattribution: the census's construct split *\"attributed every call in a plain non-static function to the\npreceding DEVICE_ACCESS -- inflating the bucket used to rank reachability\"*.  Quoting a fresh figure out of a\ntool with an open defect against the very field I am quoting would be the same error in a newer coat.  The\nreachability claim is left to the round that does the work.\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP RECORD 2026-09-07 (assess stage) -- written into state_note, NOT into `entries'. ***\n`entries' is the SEAT ANSWER list: check_stage_panels.py counts entries to decide whether a stage's\nnine seats were fired, and gen_dashboard.py REFUSES TO RENDER on a seat id no column declares.  A\nmain-loop note parked there is not merely mis-filed -- it MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER\nHAPPENED, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  Seven such entries were\nwritten during this session and all seven were migrated here when the generator refused.\n\nPREP (no code touched) -- the RE-PIN DESIGN, taken from the sibling that already did this rather than\ninvented.  `#499' re-pinned the neighbouring `other kill' row in this same probe when it removed the\ncatch-all kill, and its comment records that **BOTH obvious oracles were MEASURED VACUOUS before the third\nwas chosen**.  That is a transferable warrant, not a suggestion, because this row inverts the same way.\n\n**WHAT THE SIX ROWS ASSERT TODAY** -- `row(nm + \" ld kill\", how + (\"+txt\" if ok_txt else \"-txt\"),\n\"exit1+txt\")', i.e. the host DIES and the per-site literal `TODO: read from <sym>' appears.  Fixing the six\nread kills turns all six RED **by succeeding**, which is why the row carries the ordering obligation.\n\n**THE TWO ORACLES `#499` MEASURED VACUOUS -- do not re-propose either:**\n  * **DEATH-ONLY** (`how == \"exit1\"'): observes THAT it killed, never WHICH kill.  A message change leaving\n    `exit(1)' standing scored **35/35** against a matched control at 34/35.  Filed as [[otherkilldeath]].\n  * **`exit0`**: exit status cannot separate *declined, then quit* from *died with status 0*.  The variant\n    `mexit0' scores GREEN on an `exit0+txt' oracle while `alive=False'.  Filed as [[exit0oracle]].\n\n**THE THREE-CHANNEL SHAPE THAT SURVIVED, which the six should copy:**\n  * `how' -- the host survives;\n  * `txt' -- **ONE CONTIGUOUS literal including the address and data fields.**  Two independent substring\n    tests are a WEAKER claim: `-- declined.' is emitted by the six `#497' width declines and by `#498's\n    sgi_de decline too, so a bare substring cross-matches.  These six already have per-site symbols, which\n    is the natural discriminator;\n  * `v0' -- **a declined READ reaches the common tail and lands a DEFINED 0.**  `v0' is preset to SENTINEL\n    by `load()', so *wrote odata (0)* and *never landed* are distinguishable.  This is the channel that kills\n    `mret0' (return 0 -> memory_rw coerces to -1 -> EXCEPTION_DBE) and `mrdexit0' (exit(0) in the READ branch\n    only), **neither of which any write-only oracle can see** -- and these six ARE read arms, so this channel\n    is not optional here.\n\n**ORDER, per the oracle-correction rule:** re-pin the six FIRST, measure the COMMITTED build RED under the new\nexpectations, THEN edit `dev_sgi_re.c'.  `#499' additionally showed the stronger form -- its oracle had been\nPRE-REGISTERED six hours before its own baseline -- and this row inherits that: the design above is being\nwritten down BEFORE the round starts, in a dated entry, for exactly that reason.\n\n**Cite the sites by SYMBOL, never by line.**  The probe's own header lists `dev_sgi_re.c lines 245 262 279\n296 346 362'; `#504' inserted ~40 lines above them, so those numbers are already stale.  The probe carries a\n`SITES' table of symbolic names -- use it.\n\n*** CENSUS + RANKING, 2026-09-07 (codex, `regress/artifacts/sgirekills/codex_census_20260907.txt`).\nTHE ANSWER TO 'WHICH ARE CHEAP' IS 'NONE ARE', AND THAT IS THE USEFUL PART. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"The count is twelve.  ELEVEN ARE GUEST-REACHABLE; one is unreachable.  **There are no\ntwo or three genuine one-line repairs already covered by success tests.**  The cheapest plausible\nfunctional pair is LINEAR_A and LINEAR_B readback -- but both need corrected oracles.\"*\n\n**The count is CONFIRMED at twelve** (I re-ran the deriver before writing the brief; the title's 21\nand the intermediate 19 both counted comments).  **Eleven of twelve guest-reachable** is a stronger\nseverity statement than this row carried.\n\n*** I ASKED FOR THE CHEAP WINS AND WAS TOLD THERE ARE NONE.  Recorded as a NEGATIVE RESULT, because\nit stops the next round hunting for them. ***\n\n**THE COST RANKING, which gives a coherent round instead of a scattering:**\n\n    rank  site                        new latch bit   work\n    1     #12 dead MTE width branch   no              source cleanup only -- NO RUNTIME IMPROVEMENT\n    2=    #5  LINEAR_A read           no              replace the kill oracle with readback\n    2=    #6  LINEAR_B read           no                assertions; extend EXISTING sessions\n    3=    #1  TLB_A read              no              reuse existing sessions; add returned-value,\n    3=    #2  TLB_B read              no                packing and cell-selection assertions\n    3=    #3  TLB_C read              no\n    4=    #7  STIPPLE                 ONE             new targeted MTE rows AND session scenario\n    4=    #9  linear COPY             ONE             same\n    4=    #10 linear depth            ONE             same\n\n**RANKS 2 AND 3 ARE ONE CLASS: the five READ arms.**  No new latch bits, no new sessions, and they\nshare the oracle inversion this row already predicted -- the six `ld kill` rows in\n`regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py` that assert the host still dies.  **That is the round to run**, and it\nis the class-closure shape rather than a cell at a time: `#505` paid three floor breaches for\nrepairing cell-by-cell, and this row's sites are already grouped for me.\n\n**Rank 1 agrees with the separate `sgimtedepth4dead` audit**, which independently said DEFER and\nreduce that row to a comment correction -- two briefs, two seats, same disposition, reached from\ndifferent directions.\n\n**RANK 4 IS THE EXPENSIVE GROUP AND SHOULD NOT BE MIXED IN**: one latch bit each plus new MTE\nsessions.  Those are three separate rounds, not part of the read-arm round."
GXEMUL-SEC\tools\pipeline\ledger.json:28023:   "id": "lcabadval",
GXEMUL-SEC\tools\pipeline\ledger.json:28029:   "witness": "harness:ENUMERATED from the shipped source, 2026-08-31.  Eleven live terminating calls.  The value guards, each ending in exit(1): `idata & ~1` (:206), `idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN` (:226), `idata != 0ULL && idata != 0x300800000ULL` (:241), `idata != 0x700000ULL` (:256), `idata != 0x240000000ULL` (:280), `idata != 0x3ff00000ULL` (:295).  Each diagnostic says so in its own words -- *\"(0x%\" PRIx64\") from the only implemented value\"*.  Plus two range guards that also kill: `dev >= 21` (:129) and `ofs > len` (:163).  NO rung-3 witness: READ, not booted.",
GXEMUL-SEC\tools\pipeline\ledger.json:28101:     "outcome_note": "[HELD AWAITING SEAT: fable5]  The rung-3 seat says a witness IS constructible and specified a full sequence, and it refuted three of this row's own records (six value-guarded arms not four; eleven live exit(1) not twelve; three arms of six accept exactly one value, not one of four -- so the ORIGINAL title was closer to right than the correction that overturned it).  It also named a CONFOUND that would make the probe lie: lca_interrupt_assert and lca_interrupt_deassert are bare fatal()+exit(1) with the whole ISA PIC chain wired to them, so ANY interrupt ends the host with the SAME status as the value guards under test.  THE QUESTION FOR THE FLAGSHIP SEAT: given that every row must therefore match the fatal() TEXT rather than the exit status, and given that lcaconfwidth (filed today) means the IOC_CONF arm's text can PRINT THE WRONG VALUE, is the text-matching oracle sound at THAT arm -- or must lcaconfwidth's printf fix land FIRST, making it a prerequisite of this row rather than a sibling?  Second: the de-escalation clause owes an accept row PER PREDICATE, and the ledger's earlier census dropped W_BASE1 and W_MASK1 -- confirm the accept-row set is six, not four.",
GXEMUL-SEC\tools\pipeline\ledger.json:28102:     "note": "[HELD AWAITING SEAT: fable5]  The rung-3 seat says a witness IS constructible and specified a full sequence, and it refuted three of this row's own records (six value-guarded arms not four; eleven live exit(1) not twelve; three arms of six accept exactly one value, not one of four -- so the ORIGINAL title was closer to right than the correction that overturned it).  It also named a CONFOUND that would make the probe lie: lca_interrupt_assert and lca_interrupt_deassert are bare fatal()+exit(1) with the whole ISA PIC chain wired to them, so ANY interrupt ends the host with the SAME status as the value guards under test.  THE QUESTION FOR THE FLAGSHIP SEAT: given that every row must therefore match the fatal() TEXT rather than the exit status, and given that lcaconfwidth (filed today) means the IOC_CONF arm's text can PRINT THE WRONG VALUE, is the text-matching oracle sound at THAT arm -- or must lcaconfwidth's printf fix land FIRST, making it a prerequisite of this row rather than a sibling?  Second: the de-escalation clause owes an accept row PER PREDICATE, and the ledger's earlier census dropped W_BASE1 and W_MASK1 -- confirm the accept-row set is six, not four."
GXEMUL-SEC\tools\pipeline\ledger.json:28108:     "note": "GPT-6 Astra, max effort, on a purpose-written assess brief (answer preserved at _scratchpad/codex_lcabadval/ANSWER_astra_20260906.txt; refused the first packet on its content filter and answered the neutralised one).  **IT CORRECTED THE ROW'S CENSUS AND MY BRIEF'S, AND I VERIFIED THE SHARPEST CORRECTION BY OPENING THE FILE.**  Comment-aware counting gives **12 textual exits, 11 LIVE**: six value guards, two interrupt, one nonzero-bus, one ISA-range, one IOC-default.  **The `dev >= 21' guard my brief listed as a live range kill is COMMENTED OUT** -- dev_lca.c has `/*  fatal(...); exit(1);  */` inside it and assigns `dev = 0;` instead.  Verified.  So the brief asked about a dead guard, and the premise-audit opener is what surfaced it.  Also: exactly THREE guards accept a single value (not four), and FOUR diagnostics carry the phrase `from the only implemented'.  **IT ALSO REFUTED THE CONFOUND AS I STATED IT**, which changes the witness design rather than the defect: `Any interrupt overstates the confound' -- bus_isa.c (anchor `old_isa_assert == new_isa_assert') filters unchanged masked state, so only callbacks that actually PROPAGATE terminate.  The oracle still cannot rest on exit status, but the interference is narrower and testable.  Its witness specification: drive the named IOC cases through the real memory decode with native quadword accesses, and require same-decode liveness, a device-specific signature beyond an ordinary write/read echo, independent value and width recording, state preservation, and latch repetition plus instance controls -- with the oracle validated against separate interrupt-diagnostic fixtures.  It found NO Alpha or LCA regression probe anywhere in the tree, which is why this file has no coverage at all."
GXEMUL-SEC\tools\pipeline\ledger.json:28114:     "note": "DESIGN PASS.  VERDICT: severity order = the two INTERRUPT STUBS first, provisionally (firmware execution order unproved); PREMISE 3 IS WRONG.  *** IT ADDED A SEVENTH MEMBER TO THE DE-FATALISATION OPTION SET: IMPLEMENT. ***  All six existing members presume a refusal decision; for an unimplemented PATH none applies -- 'declining an interrupt leaves the machine incomplete'.  *** AND IT FOUND THE FAULT MEMBER IS UNAVAILABLE ON THIS MACHINE: memory_rw.c dispatches an exception on a failed device access for MIPS and M88K with NO ALPHA EQUIVALENT ***, so the dev_unreadable.c idiom does not transfer and any round reaching for FAULT owes Alpha exception delivery first.  Corrected three of my five premises: (2) the commented-out dev>=21 kill is NOT a usable precedent -- its live `dev = 0' ALIASES 'no selected device' to device zero, safe today only because slot 0 is empty; (3) W_BASE0 accepts TWO values, so only three window guards are single-value; (5) 'firmware is the only writer' is unsupported, since guest kernel stores use the same MMIO path and interrupt callbacks arise from device events.  Also flagged that the Alpha callbacks only set irq_asserted and the execution loop never consumes it, so implementing the stubs would not by itself deliver an interrupt.  Answer preserved at regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_design_20260906.txt"
GXEMUL-SEC\tools\pipeline\ledger.json:28117:   "hold_question": "The rung-3 seat says a witness IS constructible and specified a full sequence, and it refuted three of this row's own records (six value-guarded arms not four; eleven live exit(1) not twelve; three arms of six accept exactly one value, not one of four -- so the ORIGINAL title was closer to right than the correction that overturned it).  It also named a CONFOUND that would make the probe lie: lca_interrupt_assert and lca_interrupt_deassert are bare fatal()+exit(1) with the whole ISA PIC chain wired to them, so ANY interrupt ends the host with the SAME status as the value guards under test.  THE QUESTION FOR THE FLAGSHIP SEAT: given that every row must therefore match the fatal() TEXT rather than the exit status, and given that lcaconfwidth (filed today) means the IOC_CONF arm's text can PRINT THE WRONG VALUE, is the text-matching oracle sound at THAT arm -- or must lcaconfwidth's printf fix land FIRST, making it a prerequisite of this row rather than a sibling?  Second: the de-escalation clause owes an accept row PER PREDICATE, and the ledger's earlier census dropped W_BASE1 and W_MASK1 -- confirm the accept-row set is six, not four.",
GXEMUL-SEC\tools\pipeline\ledger.json:28118:   "state_note": "*** RECORDS CORRECTION 2026-09-06, from RE-DERIVING this row's own citations instead of trusting them.  THE\nHEADLINE COUNT (eleven live terminating calls) IS RIGHT.  THE ENUMERATION UNDER IT IS WRONG IN TWO WAYS, AND THE\nFIRST ONE CHANGES THE FIX DESIGN. ***\n\n**1.  `dev >= 21` IS NOT A LIVE KILL.  IT IS ALREADY DE-FATALISED -- AND IT IS THIS FILE'S OWN FIX PRECEDENT.**\n\nThe row lists it among \"two range guards that also kill\".  The source says otherwise: the guard is at `:127`, its\nbody is a COMMENTED-OUT `fatal()` + `exit(1)` at `:128-129`, and what actually executes is `dev = 0;` at `:130`.\n\n```c\n\tif (dev >= 21) {\n\t\t/*  fatal(\"[ LCA: No bus 0 device? TODO ]\\n\");\n\t\texit(1);  */\n\t\tdev = 0;\n\t}\n```\n\nSomeone has already made, in this very file, the decision this row exists to make: **delete the kill, substitute a\nsafe default, keep going.**  That is a TRANSFERRED WARRANT from inside the same file, which the six-member\noption-set rule says to go looking for -- *\"grep the fork for an existing idiom; the tree's own precedents are part\nof the design space\"* -- and which nobody had found because the row was read rather than re-derived.  So only ONE\nrange guard is live: `ofs > len` at `:163`.\n\n**2.  THE ENUMERATION COVERED 8 OF THE 11, AND THE THREE IT MISSED ARE NOT VALUE GUARDS AT ALL.**\n\nRe-derived by `grep -n 'exit(1)' dev_lca.c` minus the commented one (12 matches, 11 live), the eleven are:\n\n| line | class | condition |\n|---|---|---|\n| 73  | **INTERRUPT STUB** | `lca_interrupt_assert` -- bare `fatal(\"...TODO\"); exit(1)`, **NO GUARD** |\n| 85  | **INTERRUPT STUB** | `lca_interrupt_deassert` -- likewise |\n| 134 | BUS | `bus != 0`, i.e. `d->ioc_conf & 1` |\n| 163 | RANGE | `ofs > len` |\n| 206 | VALUE | `idata & ~1` (IOC_CONF) |\n| 226 | VALUE | `idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN` |\n| 241 | VALUE | `idata != 0 && idata != 0x300800000` |\n| 256 | VALUE | `idata != 0x700000` |\n| 280 | VALUE | `idata != 0x240000000` |\n| 295 | VALUE | `idata != 0x3ff00000` |\n| 315 | CATCH-ALL | `default:` -- any unimplemented ioc offset, read OR write |\n\n**THE TWO INTERRUPT STUBS ARE PLAUSIBLY THE MOST SEVERE OF THE ELEVEN, AND A ROW FRAMED AS A VALUE-GUARD CLASS\nCOULD NOT SEE THEM.**  They are not a wrong value being written -- they are an ENTIRE UNIMPLEMENTED PATH with no\npredicate at all, so *any* device asserting an interrupt through the LCA ends the process regardless of what it\nwrites.  The four \"accepts exactly one value\" registers need a firmware revision that writes something else; these\ntwo need only that an interrupt is asserted.\n\n*A row named after one class hides the members of the others.  That is this project's cell-not-class pattern\nwearing an enumeration instead of a detector -- the same shape the m88kexcdeliver chain hit four times.*\n\n**WHAT IS UNCHANGED:** the severity (HIGH), the single consumer (`machine_alpha.c`, `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`),\nthe absence of any alpha rig, and the observation that a guest here is FIRMWARE so the reaching value is whatever\nSRM or NetBSD writes.  The scope caveat about range-vs-value guards also stands and is now sharper, because there\nis only one live range guard to reason about.\n\n--- the original note follows, unaltered ---\n\n\n=== DESIGN PASS, 2026-09-06 (codex / GPT-6 Astra at max, read-only).  Answer preserved at\n=== regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_design_20260906.txt.  IT CORRECTED THREE OF THE FIVE PREMISES I HAD\n=== JUST RE-DERIVED, and one of the corrections changes what this round may even choose from.\n\n**SEVERITY ORDER CONFIRMED: the two INTERRUPT STUBS first** -- but \"provisionally; firmware execution order is\nunproved\", which is the honest qualifier my own framing lacked.\n\n*** A SEVENTH OPTION-SET MEMBER: IMPLEMENT. ***  The carrier's set is DELETE / DECLINE / DECLINE+LATCHED\nANNOUNCE / ACCEPT / FAULT / BACKED ACCEPT -- all six presume the access is a REFUSAL DECISION.  For the two\ninterrupt stubs none of them applies: *\"declining an interrupt leaves the machine incomplete.\"*  A stub is not a\nrefusal to service a value; it is an unimplemented PATH, and the disposition is to write the code.  This is the\noption-set completeness rule paying off exactly as it is meant to -- the member was found by a seat reading the\ntree, not by argument.\n\n*** AND THE FAULT MEMBER IS NOT AVAILABLE ON THIS MACHINE AT ALL. ***  `dev_unreadable.c` works because\n`memory_rw.c` dispatches an exception on a failed device access **for MIPS and M88K, with NO ALPHA EQUIVALENT**.\nSo the idiom this fork installs ten times in `machine_sgi.c` does not transfer here, and any round reaching for\nFAULT owes Alpha exception delivery first.  *A precedent that is real elsewhere in the tree is not thereby\ntransferable; that is the warrant-transfer rule, and it took a seat opening `memory_rw.c` to see it.*\n\n**AND A FINDING ABOUT THE ALPHA PORT THAT OUTRANKS THIS ROW:** the Alpha interrupt callbacks merely set\n`irq_asserted`, and **the execution loop never consumes that flag**.  So implementing the two stubs would not by\nitself deliver an interrupt -- removing the exits cannot complete the path.  Filed as its own question rather\nthan folded in here.\n\n## THE PREMISE AUDIT, and I was wrong three times\n\n* **PREMISE 1 HOLDS.**  Twelve textual occurrences, eleven outside comments.\n* **PREMISE 2's REMOVAL IS REAL; ITS \"USABLE PRECEDENT\" CONCLUSION IS WRONG.**  I read the commented-out\n  `dev >= 21` kill plus its live `dev = 0` as the file's own answer, transferable to this round.  It is not: it\n  **ALIASES \"no selected device\" to DEVICE ZERO**, which is harmless today only because device 7 is the sole\n  installed device and slot 0 happens to be empty.  Populate slot 0 and it is a latent alias defect.  *So the\n  question I asked -- is this a sound precedent or a latent defect of its own -- has the second answer, and my\n  brief had already leaned on the first.*\n* **PREMISE 3's CLASS COUNTS HOLD, ITS SINGLETON CLAIM DOES NOT.**  `W_BASE0` accepts **TWO** values, `0` and\n  `0x300800000` (`idata != 0ULL && idata != 0x300800000ULL`), so \"four registers accepting exactly ONE value\" is\n  wrong -- only **three** window guards accept a single value.  *This one is mine twice over: the original row\n  note said it, I re-derived the enumeration tonight and reproduced it, and the guard's own text was in my\n  terminal when I did.*\n* **PREMISE 4 HOLDS** as one machine-setup consumer (AlphaBook 1), with no dedicated boot rig -- **but\n  \"never exercised\" DOES NOT FOLLOW**: a generic constructor sweep exists (`gate_asan_sweep.sh`), without\n  proving coverage of these branches.  My \"no rig means no coverage\" was too strong.\n* **PREMISE 5 IS AN UNSUPPORTED RESTRICTION.**  I said a guest here is firmware, so the reaching value is\n  whatever SRM or NetBSD writes.  Guest KERNEL stores use the same MMIO path, and interrupt callbacks arise from\n  DEVICE events -- neither is firmware.\n\n## DISPOSITIONS IT WOULD TAKE, per class\n* **INTERRUPT STUB (2):** IMPLEMENT forwarding and CPU interrupt delivery.\n* **BUS (1):** implement Type-1 decoding then ACCEPT through `bus_pci`, using its existing absent-device\n  handling.  `ioc_conf & 1` selects the CONFIGURATION-CYCLE TYPE and is **not the decoded bus number** -- and\n  deleting this exit alone leaves `dev` uninitialised.\n* **RANGE (1):** DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE with initialised read results and no forwarded side effects.  Note\n  `ofs` is at most three, **so this guard cannot fire for four- or eight-byte accesses** -- changing `>`\n  mechanically is not a design.\n* **VALUE (6):** ACCEPT documented register fields using the existing backing, latched announce for the missing\n  DMA/TLB effects.  **Every guarded assignment already happens BEFORE its check**, so no invented backing is\n  needed -- but readback alone does not implement translation.\n* **CATCH-ALL (1):** split documented registers (`STAT0`/`STAT1`) from genuine holes; FAULT only for confirmed\n  holes and only once Alpha exception delivery exists.\n\n**WHAT IT COULD NOT ESTABLISH, said plainly:** source shows connected interrupt paths and conditional MMIO paths\nfor the other nine sites, but **no particular SRM/NetBSD execution of any terminating site**.  Firmware\nreachability and failure frequency remain unmeasured, so the severity order is provisional on exactly that.\n\n\n*** THE SEVERITY WARRANT THIS ROW WAS MISSING: THE REJECTED VALUES INCLUDE A DOCUMENTED ONE, AND THE\nSTORE LANDS BEFORE THE PROCESS ENDS.  Found by the codex seat 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt),\nverified against the source and the header before recording. ***\n\nEach of the three window guards has this shape:\n\n    d->window_mask_0 = idata;                          <- the store LANDS\n    if (idata != 0x700000ULL) { fatal(...); exit(1); } <- then the process ends\n\n**And zero is a documented encoding.**  From src/include/thirdparty/alpha_lcareg.h:\n\n    #define IOC_W_MASK_1M   0x0000000000000000ULL   /* 1MB window */\n\nSo a guest selecting the SMALLEST DOCUMENTED WINDOW SIZE ends the emulator.  The same argument covers\nW_BASE1, whose one accepted constant 0x240000000 carries IOC_W_BASE_WEN (0x200000000, *Window enable*) --\n**so a driver DISABLING a window, by writing a base with WEN clear, is killed too.**\n\n*** THIS IS #504's WARRANT AT A SECOND DEVICE: documented values that the model kills on. ***  It is not a\njunk-value guard; it is a whitelist of ONE that excludes documented members of its own encoding.  codex adds\nthat all three registers start at zero through constructor initialisation, so the rejected value is also the\nRESET value.\n\n**AND THE RANGE SITE IS FAR MORE REACHABLE THAN THIS ROW SAID.**  It was recorded as `ofs > len'.  Reading\nDEVICE_ACCESS(lca_isa): `relative_addr >>= 5; ofs = relative_addr & 3;' -- **ofs is guest ADDRESS BITS 5-6**,\nnot a written value.  So an ordinary 1-byte access at an address with those bits set to 2 or 3 ends the\nprocess.  That is address decoding, not a value whitelist, and it belongs in a different disposition class\nfrom the six value guards.  The rest of that function is filed separately as [[lcaisalane]].\n\n*** DISPOSITION PASS, codex 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_disposition_20260907.txt).  IT NARROWS MY REACHABILITY CLAIM,\nCONFIRMS TWO OTHERS, AND SUPPLIES THE IN-TREE PRECEDENTS THE OPTION-SET RULE ASKS FOR -- INCLUDING\nCONFLICTING ONES, WHICH IS WHAT I ASKED IT TO SURFACE. ***\n\nVerdict: *\"Disposition 5 is unavailable through Alpha's failed-device-access path.  Five candidate rounds fit\na uniform VALUE-refusal policy.  The tree supplies CONFLICTING precedents, especially for lane handling and\nstoring unsupported values.\"*\n\n*** MY `AN ORDINARY READ-MODIFY-WRITE ENDS THE EMULATOR' CLAIM IS TOO BROAD, AND I HAVE REPEATED IT IN THREE\nPLACES. ***  codex: *\"they read zero INITIALLY, then echo accepted writes.  Writing back the initial zero\nkills the last three; an arbitrary RMW does not necessarily kill them -- its resulting value matters.\"*\n\n**The precise claim is the INITIAL-ZERO readback**: before any accepted write, `W_MASK0', `W_BASE1' and\n`W_MASK1' read 0 and accept only one non-zero constant each, so a read-modify-write performed on a\nfreshly-reset device writes 0 back and ends the process.  **Once a valid value has been written the register\nechoes it**, and an RMW of that value is accepted.  So the window is early -- driver probe or\ninitialisation -- rather than any RMW at any time.  That is still a routine thing for a driver to do at\nattach, and it is still reached with a documented encoding (`IOC_W_MASK_1M` = 0), but the sentence I wrote\nwas wider than the fact.\n\n**CONFIRMED, both of which I had asserted:** every VALUE arm STORES BEFORE CHECKING; and the accepted sets\nare exactly as filed (`IOC_CONF' {0,1}, `IOC_TB_ENA' {0,0x80}, `W_BASE0' {0,0x300800000}, and the three\nsingle-value ones).\n\n**DISPOSITION 5 IS UNAVAILABLE -- now established from a third direction.**  The carrier already recorded it\ngenerally, [[alphafaultclaim]] reached it from the instruction side, and this reaches it from the\nfailed-device-access path.  **Any proposal for a bus-error refusal on an Alpha device owes Alpha exception\ndelivery first**, which is a far larger round than the one it would be riding on.\n\n**THE PRECEDENT I ASKED FOR, AND IT IS AN EXACT ONE:** `cpu_sh.c' defines `sh3_cpu_interrupt_assert()' and\n`sh3_cpu_interrupt_deassert()' with EMPTY `/* TODO */' bodies -- *\"an exact unimplemented-callback precedent\nfor 2, ignore silently\"* -- and `core/interrupt.c' defines empty `no_interrupt_assert/deassert' callbacks,\nthough those represent intentionally absent connections rather than unimplemented ones.  **So the two\nINTERRUPT STUBS have a direct in-tree answer** and do not need disposition 7 after all, which is what I had\nassumed they needed.\n\n**AND IT REPORTS THE PRECEDENTS CONFLICT** -- especially on lane handling and on whether to store an\nunsupported value.  That is the more useful answer: it means the choice is genuinely open at those sites\nrather than settled by the tree, so a round there must argue rather than transfer.\n\n**SCOPE: FIVE candidate rounds** under a uniform VALUE-refusal policy -- consistent with the earlier read's\n*\"this needs several rounds\"* and finer than it.\n\n**WHERE THE OVER-BROAD FORM SURVIVES, named because it cannot be edited:** commit `de96f11`'s MESSAGE\nsays *\"each reads back 0 while its guard accepts one non-zero constant, and AN ORDINARY READ-MODIFY-WRITE\nENDS THE EMULATOR on all three\"*.  A commit message is immutable, so the house rule -- correct the false\nrecord IN PLACE -- cannot be followed literally.  **The next best thing is to point at it from the record a\nreader will reach**, which is this row.  The narrowed claim above is the operative one: the kill needs the\nINITIAL zero readback, not any RMW at any time."
GXEMUL-SEC\tools\pipeline\ledger.json:28128:   "note": "*** A SURVIVAL ORACLE THAT A WRONG `exit(0)` SATISFIES IS NOT A SURVIVAL ORACLE. ***  Found by the codex seat while answering `sgideprimop`'s pass-1 brief, in shipped material it was not asked about.  `regress/sgi_de_width_probe.py` pins host survival with `row(..., how, \"exit0\", ...)` at **NINE sites across FIVE probe files**, derived rather than transcribed with `grep -rn 'how, \\\"exit0\\\"' regress/*.py`:  `sgi_de_width_probe.py` 4 (`lo_alive`, `lo_alive2`, `hi_alive`, `hi_rd_alive`), `gdrom_decline_probe.py` 2, `gdrom_default_probe.py` 1, `gdrom_toclen_probe.py` 1, `sgi_re_width_probe.py` 1.  *codex named three, in the one file it had been given; the class is three times that and spans devices it was never shown -- which is why the row is filed as a CLASS and the deriver is written down instead of the number.*  The oracle reads the process's exit status after the harness sends `quit`.  **An emulator that calls `exit(0)` on the guest access under test ALSO exits 0**, so the row cannot distinguish *the host survived and was then asked to quit* from *the host died quietly at exactly the moment the row exists to observe*.  **THE REPAIR codex NAMES:** assert the `alive` value captured BEFORE the harness sends `quit`, ideally followed by a post-trigger command whose result proves the emulator was still answering.  The probe already captures `alive` -- the rows simply do not use it.  *** RANKED AS COVERAGE DEBT, NOT AN L2, AND THE DISTINCTION IS THE DOCUMENTED ONE. ***  These rows are in a SHIPPED detector that sits above the vacuity floor: it fails on the pre-fix build, on a verbatim reinstatement (14/25, eleven reds) and on a null medium.  The stopping rule files findings against shipped material and fixes only MEASURED FALSE PASSES and WRONG RECORDS.  **No mutant has been built that exploits this**, so it is a hypothetical edit in an uncovered cell -- filed, ranked below live defects in the same file.  *What would promote it to L2: a measured mutant that calls `exit(0)` on the declined path and sweeps the detector clean.  That is one build and is the natural first job for whoever takes this row.*  **CHECK THE CLASS BEFORE FIXING THE INSTANCE** -- `grep -rn 'exit0' regress/*.py` across the tree, since the idiom will have been copied; this project has twice corrected an instance and left its siblings standing.  *** THE COUNT IN THIS ROW WAS WRONG WHEN FILED -- 9, AND THE POPULATION IS 13.  Corrected 2026-08-31 by the adjudicating seat, which could not reproduce my figure by any method. ***  **THE DERIVER, and it is the fix as well as the count:** `grep -rn '\"exit0\"' regress/*.py`, counting `row(...)` want-sites -- **13 across 5 files**: `gdrom_toclen_probe.py` 5, `sgi_de_width_probe.py` 4, `gdrom_decline_probe.py` 2, `gdrom_default_probe.py` 1, `sgi_re_width_probe.py` 1 (that one INSIDE A LOOP, so its runtime rows exceed its textual site).  **My grep was `'how, \"exit0\"'` -- anchored on the VARIABLE NAME rather than the ORACLE VALUE -- and `gdrom_toclen_probe.py` uses `how1`, `how2`, `how3`, `how4`.** Four sites invisible to a pattern that looked precise.  *A row filed to adjudicate oracle blindness shipped with a count produced by a blind pattern; that is the frozen-number rule arriving inside the filing that names it.*  *** THE RANKING SURVIVES, AND THE SEAT GAVE IT A MEASURED REASON I DID NOT HAVE: THE BLINDNESS IS PER-ROW, NOT PER-DETECTOR. ***  `lo_sent` reads `printed(t, \"v0\") if alive else \"died\"`, so it reds on ANY death whatever the exit code -- verified by the seat, which built a decline-then-`exit(0)` mutant of `#498` and found it KILLED by the read-side sentinel rows even though all four `*_alive` rows passed it.  **So no shipped detector is floor-breached as a whole (not L2), and no record is made false where a session-mate positive row exists (not L3).**  Coverage debt, filed, med -- confirmed, on evidence rather than on the stopping rule alone.  **THE FIXING ROUND'S FIRST JOB IS THEREFORE A NARROWER CENSUS: which of the 13 lack a death-proof SESSION-MATE?**  Those are the live ones; the rest are redundant belt-and-braces and can be repaired cheaply or left.  *That is a smaller and better-aimed job than \"repair 13 rows\".*\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP MEASUREMENT 2026-08-31 (not a seat answer; recorded here rather than in `entries` so it cannot be counted toward the panel). ***  evidence: grep -h 'row(.*\"exit0\"' regress/*.py | wc -l = 24 on 2026-08-31\n\n*** THE COUNT WENT STALE WITHIN HOURS OF BEING FILED, BY THE FILER'S OWN EDIT. ***  Filed 2026-08-31 as THIRTEEN; derived the SAME DAY as TWENTY-FOUR, of which sgi_re_unmapped_probe.py alone holds ELEVEN because #499 pass 4 added seven.  That is this project's documented pattern exactly -- the rounds that consult a number are the rounds that change it -- so the title now carries the DERIVER and no literal.  (Demoted: 13, filed 2026-08-31, never a current claim.)  Per-file on that date: sgi_re_unmapped 11, gdrom_toclen 5, sgi_de_width 4, gdrom_decline 2, gdrom_default 1, sgi_re_width 1.\n\nAND THE CLASS BEARS ON #499's OWN PASS-4 ROWS.  Of the twelve rows pass 4 added, five are SURVIVAL-ONLY with an `exit0` want.  The READ side is covered -- `clip rd sent` reads `died` when the host is gone.  The WRITE side has NO post-death positive observable: surv wants exit0 and is satisfied, decl already counted its message before the exit.  A WRITE-SIDE-ONLY exit(0) is therefore PREDICTED to escape all 48 rows.  That is clause (d) shaped -- guard kept, its repair (falling through to the common tail) removed -- so it is NOT filed on my reading; it is QUEUED AS MUTANT E0W and will be measured.  Floor clause (b) is unaffected and measured green: a verbatim exit(1) reinstatement scores 18/48.\n\nAND A SECOND SITE FOUND THE SAME DAY BY THE lcabadval SEAT, which makes this a CLASS rather than a probe-local habit: on `-E alpha -e alphabook1`, `lca_interrupt_assert` and `lca_interrupt_deassert` (dev_lca.c) are bare fatal()+exit(1), and bus_isa wires the whole ISA PIC chain to them.  So ANY interrupt assert or deassert kills the host with the SAME exit status as the value guards under test.  Any lcabadval row must match the fatal() TEXT INCLUDING ITS OWN DATUM, never the exit status -- an exit-status oracle there cannot tell the defect from the confound.",
GXEMUL-SEC\tools\pipeline\ledger.json:28153:   "id": "lcaconfwidth",
GXEMUL-SEC\tools\pipeline\ledger.json:30101:   "witness": "harness:source census, codex GPT-6 Astra at max, regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_irqasserted_20260906.txt",
GXEMUL-SEC\tools\pipeline\ledger.json:30102:   "state_note": "*** SEVERITY CORRECTED HIGH -> LOW THE SAME EVENING IT WAS FILED, BY THE PASS IT WAS FILED PENDING. ***  I filed this HIGH off ONE GREP while the investigation was in flight.  The census was right; the severity was not, and the correction is recorded rather than the row quietly re-graded.\n\n**VERDICT (codex, GPT-6 Astra at max): REAL GAP in all three, and ALL THREE OF MY PREMISES HOLD.**  Six families set the flag, exactly three are consumed, and alpha/i960/riscv have no reader.  There is no alternate delivery mechanism for any of the three.\n\n**BUT THE THREE ARE NOT ALIKE, AND THAT IS WHAT MOVES THE SEVERITY:**\n* **i960 -- DEAD SCAFFOLDING.**  `cpu_i960_instr.c`'s instruction switch contains ONLY `default: goto bad;`. The FIRST instruction stops as unimplemented, so no boot, no timer programming, no driver wait is ever reached.  There is no guest to miss an interrupt.  `i960_exception()` is likewise a stopping stub.\n* **riscv -- DEAD SCAFFOLDING.**  `cpu_riscv_instr.c` implements compressed NOP/ADDI only and the 32-bit branch goes unconditionally to `bad`.  `machine_test.c` DOES wire RTC -> interrupt controller -> CPU, so an asserted request really does end at the unused flag -- but this instruction subset cannot boot an OS or program that timer.\n* **alpha -- A GENUINE HOLE, and the finding is that the PORT IS INCOMPLETE.**  `cpu_alpha_palcode.c` stores entry vectors and IPL (`PAL_OSF1_wrent`, `PAL_OSF1_swpipl`) and **`kentry` has no reader anywhere**; `PAL_OSF1_rti` appears only in the naming function with no execution implementation.  So delivery is missing at the CPU/PAL level, not merely at the flag.\n\n**DISPOSITIONS:** removing the field and its stores is reasonable scaffold cleanup for i960 and riscv, and adding a dispatch check alone would achieve no usable capability in either.  For alpha the item is *track completion of the CPU/PAL/chipset path*, which subsumes `lcabadval`'s two interrupt stubs: **removing those exits still leaves both upstream forwarding and CPU delivery unfinished.**\n\n*** AND IT CORRECTED MY CENSUS IN A WAY THAT DOES NOT CHANGE THE CONCLUSION BUT DOES CHANGE THE PICTURE: `cpu_dyntrans.c` ALSO delivers MIPS and SH interrupts, through DIFFERENTLY NAMED STATE. ***  My grep keyed on one identifier and so could not see them.  That is the correlate-not-the-class shape the carrier names -- the output was a plausible number either way -- and it is exactly the risk the brief disclosed about itself.\n\n*** THE SHARPEST FINDING IS ABOUT MY BRIEF, NOT THE CODE: the seat named *\"at least one numbered premise must be wrong\"* AS THE UNSUPPORTED ASSUMPTION. ***  The three-question set closes with *WHICH IS WRONG?  Assume at least one is*, which is a good adversarial device and IS an unwarranted premise when the premises happen to be sound.  A seat that answers *none, and here is why the forcing assumption is unjustified* is giving the correct answer, and the template should say so rather than pressing for a defect that may not exist.  Filed against the template as `briefforcedwrong`.\n\nHonest limit the seat stated and this row keeps: these are source-derived consequences, with no guest boot run, and \"dead scaffolding\" means the capability is absent rather than relocated.",
GXEMUL-SEC\tools\pipeline\ledger.json:30108:     "note": "VERDICT: REAL GAP in alpha, i960 and riscv; all three premises correct as written.  Distinguished the three: i960 and riscv are DEAD SCAFFOLDING (neither can execute a second instruction -- i960's switch is only `default: goto bad;', riscv implements compressed NOP/ADDI and sends the 32-bit branch to `bad'), so there is no guest to miss an interrupt and the disposition is scaffold cleanup; alpha is a GENUINE HOLE at the CPU/PAL level (`kentry' stored with no reader, `PAL_OSF1_rti' named but unimplemented), so removing lcabadval's two interrupt exits still leaves forwarding AND delivery unfinished.  **Corrected my census**: cpu_dyntrans.c also delivers MIPS and SH interrupts through differently-named state, which a single-identifier grep could not see.  **And named my brief's forcing question as its own unsupported assumption** -- filed as briefforcedwrong.  Answer preserved at regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_irqasserted_20260906.txt"
GXEMUL-SEC\tools\pipeline\ledger.json:30120:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-06 by the codex seat, against the brief template rather than against any code.  Answering the `irqassertedunread' brief it wrote: *\"Thus the unsupported assumption is 'at least one numbered premise must be wrong.'\"*  All three premises were in fact correct.\n\n**The device earns its place and that is not in dispute** -- the carrier records it finding FOUR wrong premises in a single brief, and tonight it produced three real premise corrections on `lcabadval`.  The objection is narrower: as WORDED it instructs the seat to assume a defect exists, so a seat with sound premises must either invent one or push back on the instruction.  Pushing back is the right answer and the template does not invite it.\n\n**Proposed wording, for the adjudicator rather than applied here:** keep the question and add *`or say NONE and name the forcing assumption as the defect'*.  Held rather than edited, because the template is the artifact every brief starts from and a change to it changes every future panel -- which the carrier says is an owner decision, not a round's.",
GXEMUL-SEC\tools\pipeline\ledger.json:30130:   "state_note": "*** RAISED THE SAME EVENING IT WAS FILED, ON A READING THAT MADE IT WORSE: THE UNHANDLED VALUE IS A DOCUMENTED\nMODE, NOT A NONSENSE ONE. ***  Verified against the vendored header before this edit.\n\n`horrible_getputpixel()` extracts `tlb_mode = (mode >> 10) & 0x7` and switches on it.  The function's own\ncomment lists five NetBSD modes, and `src/include/thirdparty/crmfbreg.h` confirms their values:\n\n    DE_MODE_TLB_A  0x0000 -> 0  handled       DE_MODE_LIN_A  0x1000 -> 4  handled\n    DE_MODE_TLB_B  0x0400 -> 1  handled       DE_MODE_LIN_B  0x1400 -> 5  *** default -> *color = random() ***\n    DE_MODE_TLB_C  0x0800 -> 2  handled       (3, 6 and 7 also reach it)\n\n**FOUR OF THE FIVE DOCUMENTED MODES ARE IMPLEMENTED AND THE FIFTH RETURNS RANDOM DATA.**  So this is not a\nguest choosing an out-of-spec value; it is a guest choosing one of the five modes the vendored header defines.\n\n**THE GUEST-VISIBLE PATH, in `draw_primitive()`, and both mode words are GUEST-WRITABLE registers**\n(`CRIME_DE_MODE_SRC` / `CRIME_DE_MODE_DST` via `d->de_reg[]`):\n\n    if (drawmode & DE_DRAWMODE_XFER_EN)\n            horrible_getputpixel(0, ..., &color, src_mode);   // GET may set color = random()\n    ...\n    if (draw)\n            horrible_getputpixel(1, ..., &color, dst_mode);   // PUT writes it\n\nA failed SOURCE read and the DESTINATION write are the same `color` variable in the same loop iteration, so\nthe random value is written out per pixel with no decline.  **The code's own comment on that XFER branch reads\n*\"Pixel colors copied from another source.  (OpenBSD draws characters using this mechanism.)\"*** -- so the\nbranch is one a real guest driver uses, which is why this is filed HIGH rather than as a curiosity.\n\n**STILL OWED AND NOT CLAIMED: the rung-3 witness.**  Reachability is READ, not MEASURED -- I have shown the\nconstant exists, the extraction reaches 5, and the registers are writable, and NOT that any in-tree guest\nprograms LIN_B.  A witness-attempt brief is with codex asking exactly that, including the awkward part: the\noracle cannot pin a value because `random()` is non-deterministic, so it must pin *the destination differs\nfrom the source* and *two identical runs differ*.\n\n*** THE PROVENANCE IS THE LESSON: this came from asking what a DISABLED kill FELL BACK TO. ***  It is the\nsecond such finding today -- `lcabadval`'s disabled kill leaves a live `dev = 0` that aliases 'no selected\ndevice' to device zero.  Both look like a decision already taken and neither is one to copy.  Four exits in\nthis file are commented out; the other three still owe that question.\n\n*** RUNG-3 ATTEMPT MADE, AND THE ANSWER IS WITNESS BUILDABLE (grok).  The probe is SPECIFIED, NOT RUN --\nthat seat is plan-mode, so this is a READING and the measurement is still owed. ***\n\n**THE FALSE NEGATIVE THAT WOULD HAVE WASTED THE FIRST ATTEMPT:** at reset the destination mode is TLB_A and\n`re_tlb_a[]` has bit 31 clear, so the PUT returns WITHOUT WRITING.  A naive probe -- set SRC to LIN_B, draw,\nread the destination -- finds it unchanged and reports NO DEFECT.  Using LIN_A as the destination closes it,\nsince case 4 forces the valid bit.  `sgi_de_primop_probe.py` had already named that gap.\n\n**AND THE ORACLE IS CERTAIN, NOT PROBABILISTIC:** glibc `random()` is 31-bit, so the destination's bit 31 is\nclear and inequality with a source of `0xAABBCCDD` is guaranteed rather than 2^-32.  Two runs differ by\ndefault seeding; `-D` makes them identical, which changes the second oracle and not the defect.\n\n*** THE SEVERITY SPLIT GROK NAMED, AND IT IS THE HONEST LIMIT OF THIS ROW: `LIN_B` IS DOCUMENTED AND STORABLE\nBUT NOT SHOWN TO BE DRIVER-SELECTED. ***  It appears only in `crmfbreg.h` and this function's comment; the\nXFER comment names OpenBSD character drawing without saying which source mode; the PROM's `MTE_TLB_LIN_A/B`\nzero-fill path does NOT call `horrible_getputpixel`; and `images.md` has no O2/sgimips image to boot.  **A\ndocumented-but-unused mode and a mode a driver really selects are different severities**, and only the\nconstant's existence is established here.\n\n*** THE WITNESS IS BUILT AND MEASURED.  7/7 on the committed build, artifacts/sgirandompixel/witness_20260906/head_79cd12e.out. ***\n\nReal guest instructions on an unmodified committed `-E sgi -e ip32', no `device_add'.  Both mandated controls\ngreen: LIVENESS (store then load through RAM) and DEVICE SIGNATURE (CRIME_DE_STATUS reads 0x1e000000 where\nRAM reads 0).  The value written was 0x4b1763a1 on one run and 0x2ee4b4df on the next, **bit 31 CLEAR both\ntimes** -- glibc random() is 31-bit, so inequality with a source of 0xaabbccdd is CERTAIN rather than 2^-32,\nwhich is what makes the oracle deterministic despite the value not being.\n\n*** TWO TRAPS HIT WHILE BUILDING IT, AND THE ACCEPT CONTROL IS WHAT CAUGHT THE FIRST. ***\n* **The destination tile pointer was 0, so every pixel went to PHYSICAL ADDRESS 0.**  grok had named exactly\n  this hazard -- *'do not leave it 0: that writes phys 0'* -- and I hit it anyway from a direction it had also\n  named: a 64-bit `sd' writes the HIGH word to `[n]' and the low word to `[n+1]', so storing 0x10 gave\n  `re_linear_a[0] = 0, [1] = 0x10'.  **A probe with only the defect rows would have read the destination\n  unchanged and reported NO DEFECT.**  The ACCEPT control failed instead, which is what an accept row is for.\n* `lw` SIGN-EXTENDS into a 64-bit register, so the source value read back as 0xffffffffaabbccdd and the row\n  failed on FORMATTING rather than behaviour.  The shipped `sgi_re_width_probe.py` carries that normalisation\n  and says why; this probe should have copied it and now does.\n\n**THE ORACLE MUST INVERT AFTER THE FIX, and the order is not optional.**  These rows PASS while the defect is\nPRESENT, so post-fix they assert the wrong thing.  The oracle-correction rule requires the re-pin FIRST, the\ncommitted build measured RED under the new expectations, and the code changed only THEN -- otherwise the\nre-pin cannot be told from fitting the test to the binary.  Design panel is running; the post-fix row set\ndepends on its answer to what `*color' should be on a failed get.\n\n*** DESIGN PASS 1: THE PANEL CONVERGES ON MEMBER 3 (DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE), 3 of 5 answering seats,\nAND THE TWO DISSENTS ARE REFUTED ON A POINT BOTH FILE-READING SEATS REACHED INDEPENDENTLY. ***\n\ndeepseek and minimax voted MEMBER 5 (FAULT).  **The device access that triggers this ALREADY SUCCEEDED** --\nthe guest's store to PRIMITIVE|START was accepted -- so there is nothing left to fault, and raising a bus\nerror would report a failure for an access the hardware acknowledges.  grok put it exactly: *'member 5 is\navailable here' is true of IP32 and FALSE OF THIS CALL SITE.*  Their shared argument -- that only FAULT stops\nthe bogus write -- is true of a weaker member 3 than either file-reading seat proposed.\n\n**THE REMAINING QUESTION IS SCOPE, and it is decided by the round-scope rule rather than by vote:**\n* **codex** wants the complete fix now: an explicit success result on the function, and every GET site\n  (source, the ROP `oldcolor', MTE's `bg') taught to suppress its dependent PUT.\n* **grok** wants the minimal one: delete the `random()' assignment, let the PUT write `fg', and file skip-PUT\n  and a real LIN_B case as separate rounds.\n**A return value plus seven call sites is not ONE SITE**, and the reopening rule says a round takes one.\ngrok's scoping is taken; codex's obligations are filed rather than dropped.\n\n*** THE ORACLE IS GROK'S AND IT DISCRIMINATES EVERY CANDIDATE AT ONCE, which is why it is worth having even\nthough the fix is minimal: *** zero leaves `0`, skip-PUT leaves the pre-paint `0x11111111`, IMPLEMENT-as-copy\nleaves `0xaabbccdd`, and untouched-with-PUT leaves `fg`.  **THE PROBE MUST THEREFORE PROGRAM `fg` TO A\nDISTINCTIVE NON-ZERO VALUE** -- the witness ran with `fg=0x0`, where 'untouched' and 'zeroed' are the same\nobservation and the oracle would be vacuous.  `lin_b written` is DROPPED as a defect row: under this member it\nis true before and after.\n\n*** PASS 2: SHIP, SIX SEATS ANSWERING, NONE WALLED, NONE DOWN -- the fullest roster of the session, and it cost one liveness call to get there. ***\n\nThree explicit SHIP verdicts (kimi, glm, minimax); both FILE-READING seats answered without a verdict line and both were read in full, which is where the one finding was.  **codex re-derived all 40 row outcomes (8 rows x 5 mutants) from the preserved artifacts and confirmed the table**, saying plainly it ran nothing itself.  **grok answered NONE IS WRONG to the five-way question and named the question's own frame as the unsupported assumption** -- the second seat tonight to make that correction independently.\n\n**THE ONE L4, AND IT WAS MINE:** `SGIRE_W_PRIM_OP`'s comment still claimed `1 << 10` was the next free bit and that the enum ended there -- false the moment `SGIRE_W_BADMODE = 1 << 10` was appended below it.  The file warns about exactly this collision four lines earlier, having paid for it once; I read that warning, placed my bit correctly by it, and left the signpost pointing at the bit I had taken.  Fixed in-round:\nthe superseded sentence is DEMOTED inside its own correction, so exactly one live next-free claim remains and it sits on the last enumerator.\n\n**WHAT BOTH FILE-READING SEATS AGREE IS NOT COVERED**, recorded as the contract's disclosure rather than discovered later: line and MTE execution paths, other mode combinations, junk modes 3/6/7, the dest-side LIN_B PUT, MTE's `&bg' GET, the colour-mode default arm, and resistance to latch-sharing mutants (disclosed 8/8 in isolation, because nothing in these sessions sets the other bit first).\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP RECORD 2026-09-06 (assess stage) -- written into state_note, NOT into `entries'. ***\n`entries' is the SEAT ANSWER list: check_stage_panels.py counts entries to decide whether a stage's\nnine seats were fired, and gen_dashboard.py REFUSES TO RENDER on a seat id no column declares.  A\nmain-loop note parked there is not merely mis-filed -- it MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER\nHAPPENED, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  Seven such entries were\nwritten during this session and all seven were migrated here when the generator refused.\n\nReading, not measurement: verified the default arm executes `*color = random()`, that LIN_B extracts to tlb_mode 5 and is unhandled while the other four documented modes are handled, that both mode words come from guest-writable de_reg entries, and that the failed source GET and the destination PUT share one `color` variable in one loop iteration.  Rung-3 witness attempt fired at codex.\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP RECORD 2026-09-06 (assess stage) -- written into state_note, NOT into `entries'. ***\n`entries' is the SEAT ANSWER list: check_stage_panels.py counts entries to decide whether a stage's\nnine seats were fired, and gen_dashboard.py REFUSES TO RENDER on a seat id no column declares.  A\nmain-loop note parked there is not merely mis-filed -- it MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER\nHAPPENED, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  Seven such entries were\nwritten during this session and all seven were migrated here when the generator refused.\n\nWITNESS BUILT AND GREEN, 7/7 on HEAD 79cd12e, from grok's specification.  Its design credit is specific: the warning that a naive probe finds nothing because the reset destination mode has its valid bit clear was the difference between a witness and a false negative -- and the accept control it implied is what caught my own instance of that trap.  Probe frozen at artifacts/sgirandompixel/witness_20260906/probe.frozen.py (md5 a734e524).  Kill table NOT started: the fix design comes first, because the oracle has to invert and the oracle-correction rule fixes the order.\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP RECORD 2026-09-07 (regress stage) -- written into state_note, NOT into `entries'. ***\n`entries' is the SEAT ANSWER list: check_stage_panels.py counts entries to decide whether a stage's\nnine seats were fired, and gen_dashboard.py REFUSES TO RENDER on a seat id no column declares.  A\nmain-loop note parked there is not merely mis-filed -- it MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER\nHAPPENED, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  Seven such entries were\nwritten during this session and all seven were migrated here when the generator refused.\n\nGATE WIRING -- and **the gate's first run FAILED on the block I had just written**, which is the padded-column\ntrap in its MATCHES-TWO-ROWS direction: `FAIL sgi_re randompixel row: lin_b diag  got=2 want=1` while the probe\nemitted 8 rows, 8 green, 0 red.  The probe was right and the CHECK was wrong -- `\"^$v .*ok$\"` with ONE space\nmatches `lin_b diag once' as well as `lin_b diag'.  **The sibling block eight lines above uses TWO spaces**; I\ncopied its comments and its two lessons and not its pattern.\n\nFixed and PRE-FLIGHTED against the real probe log before re-running the gate -- the two-second check the carrier\nprescribes for this exact trap.  Both directions measured: the fixed pattern selects exactly 1 per row (SUM=8),\nand the negative control still reproduces `got=2` under the old form.\n\n**The margin is recorded in the comment because the other direction is SILENT.**  The probe prints \"%-16s\" plus\nan explicit space, so a name of N chars is followed by (16-N)+1 spaces; the longest here is `lin_b diag once' at\n15, leaving exactly two.  A name of 16+ makes the two-space pattern UNSATISFIABLE and the check stays GREEN while\ntesting nothing.\n\n*** CLOSED 2026-09-07 in commit 257f56f -- AND SECTION L HAD TO TELL ME, WHICH IS THE POINT. ***\n\n`precommit_check.sh` section L refused the NEXT commit with:\n\n    HARD   257f56f   sgirandompixel open   work shipped and the row does not name it (commits EMPTY)\n\n**That is the UNRECORDED ROUND failure `BRIEF_TEMPLATE` section C0 exists to prevent** -- a round ships, the\nledger freezes at its pre-ship state, and a later loop treats the row as unfinished and re-panels it.  The\ntemplate records that costing NINE SEATS on `dev_wdc.c`.\n\n**I wrote about that failure in this same session and then committed it one commit later.**  The gate caught\nit in the very next precommit, which is exactly the difference between a rule in prose and a rule in a check:\nthe `#488` instance went undetected until a full panel had been convened on work that had already shipped.\n\nRecorded here rather than fixed silently, because a row that quietly gains a SHA looks the same afterwards\nwhether the author noticed or the gate did.",
GXEMUL-SEC\tools\pipeline\ledger.json:30364:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07, found while writing the design brief for `sgirandomcolour' -- i.e. by reading the arm NEXT\nto the one grok pointed at.  **This is a bigger defect than the row that led me to it, and it needs no\nundocumented mode at all.**\n\n`horrible_getputpixel()' declares `uint8_t buf[8];' and NEVER INITIALISES IT.  Verified: no memset in the\nfunction, no initialiser, and the only memsets in the file are the device struct, `rop_used' and `zerobuf'.\nThe two fields that decide how much of `buf' is USED are decoded from the SAME guest-written mode register\nand are INDEPENDENT of each other:\n\n    uint32_t color_mode = mode & DE_MODE_TYPE_MASK;      /* bits 4-7  */\n    int bufdepth = 1 << ((mode >> 8) & 3);               /* bits 8-9  -> 1, 2, 4 or 8 */\n\nThe colour arms fill a number of bytes fixed by the COLOUR type; the transfer length is `bufdepth'.  Nothing\nanywhere makes them agree -- `grep -rn '>> *8) *& *3' src/devices/' finds the decode, its own #100 comment,\nand the XFER siblings; NO GUARD.\n\n**TWO REACHABLE UNINITIALISED PATHS, IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS:**\n\n* **PUT -- host stack INTO guest-visible memory.**  With the depth field = 3 (`bufdepth' 8) NO colour arm\n  writes `buf[4..7]' -- not one of the five, including the correct ones -- and all eight bytes go straight to\n  `memory_rw(..., tileptr + ofs, buf, bufdepth, MEM_WRITE, NO_EXCEPTIONS | PHYSICAL)'.  `DE_MODE_TYPE_CI'\n  writes only `buf[0]', so CI at any depth above 1 leaks the rest.\n* **GET -- host stack INTO `*color', which the caller then STORES.**  The read fills only `bufdepth' bytes,\n  then RGB/RGBA/ABGR combine `buf[0..3]'.  Depth field 0 (`bufdepth' 1) with `DE_MODE_TYPE_RGBA' reads ONE\n  byte from memory and builds a 32-bit colour from four.  `draw_primitive()' writes that to the destination\n  in the same loop iteration -- **the identical caller-consumes-a-failed-read shape `sgirandompixel' just\n  fixed one arm of**, reached here with every colour mode documented and implemented.\n\n*** THE #100 COMMENT IS THE TELL, AND IT IS ON THE DECLARATION ITSELF. ***  It reads\n`/*  #100: bufdepth can be 8 = 1<<((mode>>8)&3)  */' -- so a previous round worked out that the guest can\ndemand 8 bytes and GREW THE ARRAY to match, which fixed a stack OVERFLOW.  **It did not ask what fills the\nbytes it had just added.**  The array is the right size and the contents are undefined.\n\nSEVERITY: guest-reachable read of uninitialised host stack whose value becomes guest-visible.  In a loop the\nstale bytes are usually the PREVIOUS pixel's, so the everyday symptom is wrong colours; on the first call in\na frame they are whatever the host stack held.  Both mode words are ordinary device registers reached by\nordinary stores, and all seven call sites pass one of them.\n\nNOT YET WITNESSED -- filed at once so the finding is durable.  The witness is expected to be cheap because\n`regress/sgi_re_randompixel_probe.py' already programs this exact path on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32' and\nneeds only a different mode word: its `MODE_LIN_A = 0x1220' is LIN_A | depth-field 2 (`bufdepth' 4) | RGBA,\nwhich is the MATCHED case and leaks nothing.  `0x1020' is the same line with depth field 0.\n\n*** PASS-1 CORRECTION FROM THE GROK SEAT, VERIFIED AGAINST THE HEADER BEFORE BEING ACCEPTED.  MY PATH-A\nEXEMPLAR WAS THE WEAKER ONE, AND A FULLY DOCUMENTED VEHICLE EXISTS. ***\n\nThe brief drove PATH A with mode `0x1320' -- depth field 3, `bufdepth' 8 -- and called it reachable.  grok:\n*\"BUFDEPTH in the header is 8/16/32 only -- field 3 is undocumented ... PATH A is live on documented `CI' +\n`BUFDEPTH_16/32' (`0x1200'), not only on undocumented depth 8.  The brief's `0x1320' PATH-A vehicle is the\nweaker one.\"*\n\n**CHECKED, not taken on trust** -- `grep -n BUFDEPTH src/include/thirdparty/crmfbreg.h' returns exactly three:\n\n    DE_MODE_BUFDEPTH_8   0x00000000   -> depth field 0 -> bufdepth 1 byte\n    DE_MODE_BUFDEPTH_16  0x00000100   -> depth field 1 -> bufdepth 2 bytes\n    DE_MODE_BUFDEPTH_32  0x00000200   -> depth field 2 -> bufdepth 4 bytes\n\n**Field value 3 is documented nowhere**, so the brief's own vehicle relies on an undocumented depth -- while\n`DE_MODE_TYPE_CI' assigns ONLY `buf[0]' and `DE_MODE_BUFDEPTH_32' transfers FOUR bytes, so `0x1200' leaks\n`buf[1..3]' with **every field documented and CI being colour type ZERO**, the reset value.\n\n*(Note the naming trap I had not spotted: the constants are in BITS and the code's `bufdepth' is in BYTES, so\n`BUFDEPTH_32' is 4.  That is why `1 << ((mode >> 8) & 3)' maxes at 8 with only three constants defined.)*\n\n**AND IT SETTLED Q1.3, WHICH I HAD EXPLICITLY LEFT OPEN: the destination is RAM, not a device.**  So `#95's\ndevice-read pre-zero does not run AT ALL on either path -- not merely `does not cover the bytes past len'.\nBoth paths are therefore worse than the brief stated, not better.\n\n**This is the premise-audit opener earning its place for the third recorded time.**  I asked seats to attack\nthe reachability premise; one did, and the correction STRENGTHENS the finding while WEAKENING the exemplar I\nhad chosen -- which is exactly the asymmetry the rule exists to surface.\n\ngrok's other two calls -- **do not join [[sgirandomcolour]]** (my Q6) and **talk HIGH down to MEDIUM** (my Q5)\n-- are NOT actioned here.  They are adjudication questions and the panel is still running; the\nprovisional-adjudication rule says a ruling short of the roster is provisional on its face, so they wait for\nthe full six.\n\n*** PASS-1 BRIEF DEFECT, RAISED INDEPENDENTLY BY ALL THREE PACKET-FED SEATS: I ASSERTED A PREMISE THEY\nCOULD NOT CHECK, AND SAID `SETTLED PREMISES: NONE CARRIED'. ***\n\nNo burns -- all three returned real verdicts on a 31 KB brief -- and all three named the same hole:\n  * **minimax**: *\"the load-bearing premise is NOT CHECKABLE and the brief states it confidently anyway ...\n    That single fact -- the body of `cpus/memory_rw.c' -- is what would settle it, and the brief should not\n    have stated it as confidently as it did.\"*\n  * **glm**: *\"reachability rests on one uncheckable premise\"*; it treated every grep and scan output in the\n    brief as uncheckable and said so.\n  * **deepseek**: caught a SECOND one I had not noticed -- *\"the brief's table for `default'/YCRCB assumes\n    `fatal()' returns\"*.  My PATH-A table lists the `default:' arm as filling `buf[0..3]' and then writing;\n    **if `fatal()' exited, that arm would never reach the write and the row would be wrong.**\n\n**BOTH RESOLVE IN THE FINDING'S FAVOUR, WHICH IS NOT THE POINT.**  Checked at HEAD: `fatal()' in\n`src/core/debugmsg.c' is `va_start / va_debug / va_end' with **no exit and no early-out**, so the arm does\nreach the write; and grok, a FILE-READING seat, settled the other by reporting the destination is RAM rather\nthan a device, so `#95's pre-zero never runs at all.\n\n**THE DEFECT IS THAT I MADE A DOCUMENTED MISTAKE THE TEMPLATE WARNS ABOUT IN CAPITALS.**  `BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md`\nsection C is headed *\"MANDATORY.  COPY FORWARD.  THIS IS THE ONE THAT GETS DROPPED\"*, and my brief wrote\n*\"SETTLED PREMISES: none carried -- first brief of this campaign\"*.  **That was true about the CAMPAIGN and\nfalse about the QUESTIONS**: the template's own test is whether a seat could build an *if X then A, else B*\nanswer on the premise -- and deepseek built exactly that on `fatal()'.  The obligation is PER-PACKET and\nBROAD, *whatever pass or round settled it*, and `fatal()'-does-not-exit was settled long ago; the carrier\nrecords it costing the minimax seat its answer twice before.\n\n**THE TRANSPORT PREAMBLE DID ITS JOB AND THE BRIEF DID NOT.**  `panel_ollama.py' asks a packet-fed seat to\nNAME any premise it cannot settle instead of guessing, and all three did precisely that rather than\nratifying -- which is the behaviour that makes a packet-fed seat worth firing.  Score this as a BRIEF DEFECT,\nnever as three weak answers.\n\n**FOR THE PASS-2 PACKET:** inline (a) `fatal()'s four-line body, (b) the `memory_rw' pre-zero site with the\nbranch that gates it, and (c) grok's RAM-not-device finding with its warrant.  All three are one or two lines\neach and they are what these seats spent their answers asking for.\n\n*** THE CLASS SCAN'S DENOMINATOR IS WRONG, AND THE WAY IT IS WRONG IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING.  Codex seat,\npass 1, verdict GO-WITH-CHANGES. ***\n\nThe brief claimed SIX unpinned-length sites and FIVE stack-buffer write sites, and concluded that\n`dev_sgi_re.c' is the only member of the class in the device tree.  **Codex found four write sites both of my\nscanners missed**, and named the mechanism for each:\n\n  * `dev_dec21143.c' descriptor writebacks (two sites) -- *\"Nested argument commas defeat the scanner's\n    splitting.\"*  **That is limit 1, which the brief DISCLOSED**, demonstrated on real sites.\n  * `dev_dreamcast_maple.c' response writes (two sites) -- *\"whose casts prevent recognition of the buffer\n    argument.\"*  **That limit was NOT disclosed and I did not know about it.**\n\nAll four are SAFE (initialised via `store_le32', or cast forms that do fill the buffer), **so the CONCLUSION\nsurvives and the DENOMINATOR does not.**  Those are different claims and the brief conflated them.\n\n*** THE METHODOLOGICAL POINT IS SHARPER THAN THE COUNT, AND IT LANDS ON THE BRIEF'S BEST-LOOKING EVIDENCE: ***\n*\"Agreement between scanners sharing parsing limitations is not exhaustive corroboration.\"*  The brief\npresented TWO independent scans agreeing as strong evidence of scope.  **They are not independent** -- both\nsplit `memory_rw' arguments on commas with the same regex, so both are blind to the same call shapes.  Two\nderivers that can fail the same way are ONE deriver run twice, and this project has already recorded that\nexact lesson on the `sgirekills' count, where two agreeing greps both counted comment lines.\n\n**Two of the brief's SAFE verdicts also need narrowing, per the same seat:**\n  * **GBE** -- the divisor is nonzero and the transfer is bounded, but *\"conversion still loops to 512 after a\n    shorter read\"*, which can consume unfilled bytes.  Codex declines to call it a demonstrated guest\n    disclosure (the forwarded framebuffer prefix is smaller) but says it *\"invalidates the blanket\n    initialization-safety claim\"*.  Correct: my check established the LENGTH bound and said nothing about\n    consumption.\n  * **PXG** -- `#207' bounds the later SRAM copy's SOURCE SPAN without bounding CONSUMPTION, and the initial\n    SRAM copy is unchecked.  The enumerated 3080-byte PX maximum stands; what falls is treating it as proof of\n    complete initialisation across BOTH branches.\n\n**Consequence for this round: the scope sentence must be rewritten before pass 2** -- from *\"the only member\nof this class in the device tree\"* to *\"the only DEFECTIVE member among the sites these two scanners can\nsee, whose blind spots are nested-comma argument lists and cast buffer arguments\"*.  The two new correctness\nfindings the same seat raised are filed separately as [[sgirergbroundtrip]] and [[sgimtedepth4dead]].\n\n*** PASS-1 ADJUDICATION.  EVIDENCE BASIS: 6 of 9 -- six scriptable seats ANSWERED (0 walled, 0 down, 0\nburns, novelty 93-100%); agy DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED on the measurement reserve; opus5 and fable5 HELD for the\n2026-09-09 adjudication by standing owner directive.  The three absences are DIRECTED, not failures -- but the\nprovisional-adjudication rule still applies and this ruling is provisional on its face. ***\n\n**THE FINDING SURVIVES ALL SIX.**  Nobody disputed that `buf' is uninitialised, that the two mode fields are\nindependent, or that `#95' fails to cover the paths.  What changed is the FRAMING, in three places, and every\ncorrection came from a seat rather than from me.\n\n**D1 -- Q6, ONE ROUND OR TWO: SEPARATE.  My proposal REFUTED, and refuted with a measurement.**  Three seats\nsaid separate (grok, kimi, deepseek); kimi proved it: a shared *destination is deterministic across runs*\noracle is **VACUOUS for this row**, because `srandom(tv.tv_sec ^ getpid() ^ tv.tv_usec)' makes `random()' vary\nper run while STALE-STACK REUSE UNDER A FIXED GUEST PROGRAM IS RUN-DETERMINISTIC -- so the buggy build PASSES.\nVerified at `src/core/main.c:688-691'.  **I asked the panel not to let the convenience of one round drive the\nanswer, and it did not.**  ACCEPTED: two rounds.\n\n**D2 -- Q5, SEVERITY: grok says MEDIUM, kimi says HIGH *conditionally*.  RESOLVED BY KIMI'S CONDITION, not by\ncounting.**  kimi: HIGH is defensible for this fork's mandate *'provided the entry carries the corrected\nreachability framing ... Do not ship the entry with \"no undocumented value anywhere\" in it.'*  The row KEEPS\nHIGH and the framing is corrected here.  grok's MEDIUM rests on disclosure impact, which is genuinely weak (4\nstale bytes per pixel, usually prior pixels); kimi's HIGH rests on the fork's mandate and on this defect\nfalsifying `#95's *closes the whole class* sentence in the same batch that edited this array.  **That is the\nstronger warrant for THIS fork**, and it is the one I take.  Recorded as a SPLIT, not as agreement.\n\n**F1 -- A FALSE SENTENCE IN MY BRIEF, and it is NOT being edited.**  The brief says the paths are reached\n*'with no undocumented value anywhere'*.  **False**: PATH A's MAXIMAL form (`bufdepth' 8) needs the\nundocumented depth field 3.  Documented forms exist -- CI x BUFDEPTH_16/32 for PATH A, and PATH B entirely --\nso the FINDING stands while the blanket phrase does not.  **The sent brief is left BYTE-UNCHANGED on purpose:\nit is the frozen packet six seats reviewed, and rewriting it would falsify the record of what was asked.**\nThe correction lives here and goes into the pass-2 packet.\n\n**F2 -- THE WITNESS IS NOT `ONE CONSTANT'.**  kimi: it needs a register plus the `step_x' twin, or ZERO extra\nregisters via a PUT-only RECT path.  My cost claim was optimistic; the PUT-only path is the cheaper design and\nwas not in the brief at all.\n\n**F3 -- INDEPENDENT CONVERGENCE ON A NEW DEFECT.**  codex and kimi BOTH found the dead `if (depth == 4)'\nwithout knowledge of each other.  Independent convergence is the one kind of agreement that IS evidence, so\n[[sgimtedepth4dead]] ships with two witnesses to its existence.\n\n**OWED BEFORE PASS 2**, from §C2 and the three packet-fed seats who each asked for it: inline `fatal()'s body,\nthe `memory_rw' pre-zero site with its gating branch, and grok's RAM-not-device finding with its warrant.\n\n*** UNDER-HARVESTED REVIEW, CORRECTED 2026-09-07.  I READ THIS SEAT'S ANSWER FOR ITS NEW FINDINGS AND\nSTOPPED.  IT ALSO REFUTED FOUR OF MY CLAIMS AND **REJECTED MY WITNESS DESIGN**, AND I RECORDED NONE OF IT.\nTHIS IS THE UNREAD-REVIEW CLASS, ON AN ANSWER I HAD ALREADY OPENED. (regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/pass1_panel/codex.txt) ***\n\n**1. MY WITNESS DESIGN IS REJECTED, and the reason is architectural rather than a detail.**  I proposed\npainting a first pixel so `buf' holds KNOWN bytes, then reading a second at a mismatched depth and asserting\nthe result carries bytes from the first.  codex: *\"Separate automatic objects have no architectural promise\nof retaining each other's contents.  Register allocation, inlining, and intervening calls matter.\"*  And the\nsame objection kills the other direction: *\"**Path A has the same problem:** observing an unassigned byte\ndoes not guarantee that its value differs from the intended padding.\"*  **I flagged that risk myself in the\nbrief -- `an oracle of \"the value is wrong\" can pass by luck' -- proposed a fix for it, and did not record\nthat the fix was refused.**\n\n**The prescribed measurement instead: an ATTRIBUTED INITIALIZEDNESS RESULT per direction** -- MemorySanitizer\nor Valgrind Memcheck -- *\"tied to the reviewed build, together with passing functional controls\"*, with the\ncaveat that *\"merely copying undefined bytes need not produce an immediate diagnostic\"*, so instrumentation\ncoverage must be established rather than assumed.  **That is a different kind of round from the one I was\nplanning** and it is why this row is not next in the queue.\n\n**2. `LIN_A cannot be refused' IS FALSE.**  I wrote that case 4 FORCES the valid bit so the arm is reached\nunconditionally.  codex: *\"remove 'LIN_A cannot be refused': its index guard can return before the buffer\ncode.\"*  `tile_nr = x >> 12; if (tile_nr >= 32) return;` -- an early return I read past.\n\n**3. `THE WITNESS IS ONE CONSTANT CHANGED' IS FALSE.**  *\"The existing probe uses its LIN_A constant for the\ndestination and matched control as well as a source case.  Changing that shared constant changes the\nobservation path and the control simultaneously.\"*  My cost estimate was wrong for the same reason kimi's\ncorrection said so, and I recorded kimi's and not this one.\n\n**4. `UNINITIALISED ON EVERY PATH' OVERSTATES IT.**  *\"matched operations initialize every byte they\nconsume.\"*  The defect is the MISMATCHED pairs, not the buffer in general.\n\n**5. MY `WORSE THAN I SAID' CLAIM ABOUT RAM IS WRONG.**  I recorded that grok's RAM-not-device finding makes\nboth paths worse.  codex: *\"**RAM does not inherently make Path B worse:** a successful RAM read defines the\nrequested prefix through `memcpy'; the missing suffix is the same problem.\"*\n\n**6. NOT ALL SEVEN CALL SITES TAKE THE GUEST MODE WORDS** -- *\"the two MTE sites construct their own modes\"*.\nMy reachability paragraph implies all seven.\n\n**7. SEVERITY: MED, not HIGH.**  *\"HIGH is not established by inheriting #95's severity.\"*  And it refuses\nthe downgrade too: *\"'usually the previous pixel' and 'first call in a frame' are unsupported explanations of\nstack contents.  They cannot justify downgrading this to a cosmetic rendering defect.\"*  **Both of my\nseverity arguments were unsupported, in opposite directions.**  Lowered to MED provisionally, with potential\nhost information disclosure recorded as unestablished rather than assumed either way.\n\n*** THE PROCESS FAILURE IS THE POINT AND IT IS THE THIRD INSTANCE TONIGHT. ***  I opened this file, extracted\nthe two NEW defects and the census correction, and stopped -- because new findings are what I was looking\nfor.  **A seat that refutes your premises is easy to under-read precisely because its corrections are not\nphrased as discoveries.**  The same failure hit the `lcabadval` answer (an eleventh site missed) and the\n`alphaswpctxptbr` premises.  All three were answers already on disk.\n\n*** THE STRONGEST REACHABILITY VEHICLE WAS IN THE KIMI ANSWER ALL ALONG, AND IT IS ALL-DOCUMENTED.\nHarvested and verified 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/pass1_panel/kimi.txt). ***\n\nEverything above argues reachability from the DE path and concedes that PATH A's maximal form needs the\nundocumented depth field 3.  **There is a third vehicle that needs nothing undocumented at all**, and it is\nthe one a real driver uses: `do_mte_transfer()' constructs its own modes with NO colour bits (so CI), because\nthe `if (depth == 4)' that would set RGBA is dead code -- see [[sgimtedepth4dead]], raised to HIGH on this.\n\nCI fills only `buf[0]'; `MTE_DEPTH_16' and `MTE_DEPTH_32' are documented and give `bufdepth' 2 and 4.  **So\nevery MTE fill at those depths leaks 1 or 3 uninitialised bytes per pixel, with every value documented**, on\nthe path kimi names as used by `crmfb_fill_rect'.\n\n**This also settles the `not all seven call sites take the guest mode words' correction** recorded above from\nthe codex seat: the two MTE sites construct their own modes -- and constructing them is exactly what makes\nthem leak.  The two corrections are the same fact seen from opposite sides, and I recorded one without\nnoticing it implied the other.\n\n**AND BOTH FILE-READING SEATS INDEPENDENTLY REACHED THE DETECTOR REQUIREMENT:** the oracle must assert\nVALUES, not determinism.  kimi's proof is the sharper one -- post-fix PATH B yields a deterministic value AND\nthe `bufdepth'-sized mutant yields the previous pixel's bytes, which are *also* deterministic under stack\nreuse, so *\"a determinism oracle CANNOT catch the mutant\"*.  Two seats, two routes, one conclusion.\n\n*** FOURTH UNDER-HARVESTED ANSWER, AND THE RICHEST PER BYTE: THE GROK SEAT'S 15 KB\n(regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/pass1_panel/grok.txt).  IT ANSWERED THE OPTION-SET QUESTION WITH GREPPED IN-TREE PRECEDENT, NAMED\nTHREE MEMBERS I HAD NOT, ARGUED THE DISPOSITION I WAS LEANING TOWARD IS WRONG, AND FOUND THE MTE CHAIN\nINDEPENDENTLY.  I HAD RECORDED ONLY ITS BUFDEPTH CORRECTION. ***\n\n**1. THE MTE CHAIN, INDEPENDENTLY.**  *\"16- and 32-bit MTE TLB fills therefore always run as CI + matching\nbufdepth.  32-bit fill writes `bg''s low byte plus three uninit bytes.  Comment at :1322 says NetBSD\n`crmfb_fill_rect' uses this.\"*  **So TWO seats found it and I missed it from both** -- and grok additionally\npinned the in-file comment naming the driver, which I had attributed to kimi alone.\n\n**Its fix-order point is sharper than the one I recorded:** *\"Do NOT join: fixing `== 4' to `== 32' changes\nMTE 32-bit COLOUR semantics, which memset does not.  Memset would turn that path into 'low byte + zeros' --\ndefined, still wrong.\"*  I had noted that repairing the dead branch alone MASKS the leak; grok's is the dual\nand it matters as much -- **the memset fix leaves the MTE path DEFINED AND STILL WRONG**, because the colour\nsemantics are a separate defect from the initialisation.  Neither fix subsumes the other.\n\n**2. THREE OPTION-SET MEMBERS I DID NOT NAME**, and it reached them by grepping rather than reasoning:\n    6.  `uint8_t buf[8] = {0};`  -- the C99 form, same observable as a memset\n    7.  CLAMP THE TRANSFER to bytes filled (`len = min(bufdepth, filled)`) -- guest-visible difference: the\n        OLD DESTINATION BYTES SURVIVE, which is neither zeros nor stale stack\n    8.  IMPLEMENT the modes (YCRCB, 64-bit depth) -- and it refused this itself on the ADDITION LICENCE: no\n        CRIME DE pixel-packing datasheet exists in `_scratchpad/`\n\n**3. THE IN-TREE PRECEDENTS, WHICH IS WHAT THE OPTION-SET RULE ASKS FOR AND I DID NOT DO:**\n    * **this function's own RGB PUT already pads with zero** (`buf[3] = 0`) -- in-function precedent for\n      DEFINED PADDING rather than refusal;\n    * `dev_dreamcast_maple.c' does `memset(buf, 0, 8)' then a partial fill then `MEM_WRITE' -- **the same\n      shape, and the house answer there is zero the whole array**;\n    * `dev_sgi_gbe.c' (`#196') renders an unimplemented colour type as BLACK -- defined zeros, no `fatal'\n      flood;\n    * MTE's own `zerobuf' is memset before its write, in this same file.\n\n**4. IT ARGUES DECLINE IS THE WRONG DISPOSITION, AND THE REASON TIES BACK TO THE MTE CHAIN:**\n*\"`CI' + `BUFDEPTH_32' is a plausible 8-bit index in a 32-bit container (RGB already pads).  Declining it\nwould also break the MTE 16/32-bit TLB fill path, which ALREADY EMITS THAT PAIR.  No hardware evidence it is\nillegal.\"*  **That is decisive against member 4** and I had not connected the two facts -- the very pair a\ndecline would reject is the one the model's own MTE path generates.\n\nIts recommendation is **(1)/(6), full-object zero**, with the comment saying plainly that hardware padding is\nunknown and this is defined behaviour rather than a CRIME model -- which is also what the codex seat\nindependently chose.\n\n*** 5. AN APPARENT DISAGREEMENT I COLLAPSED, RESOLVED HERE BY READING BOTH RATHER THAN PICKING ONE. ***\nI recorded codex's *\"RAM does not inherently make PATH B worse\"* as a correction of my summary.  grok says\nthe opposite in its heading: *\"PATH B is worse than the brief said.\"*  **Reading both: they are not in\nconflict.**  grok's point is that `#95' does not run AT ALL on a RAM destination, so nothing pre-zeroes\nanything; codex's is that a successful RAM read still DEFINES the requested prefix through `memcpy', so only\nthe suffix beyond `bufdepth' is undefined -- which is the same defect either way.  **codex is right on the\ntechnical point and grok is right about the brief's framing.**  Recording only one of them, as I did, loses\nthat.\n\n*** THE THREE PRECEDENTS VERIFIED, AND THE THIRD IS THIS PROJECT'S OWN PRIOR RULING ON THE SAME\nQUESTION.  Checked 2026-09-07 -- grok said `grep, not argument', so they were grepped. ***\n\n    buf[3] = 0;                         dev_sgi_re.c, this function's own RGB PUT      CONFIRMED\n    memset(buf, 0, 8);                  dev_dreamcast_maple.c, twice (:285, :330)      CONFIRMED\n    *r = *g = *b = 0;                   dev_sgi_gbe.c #196                             CONFIRMED\n\n**The `#196' comment is the decisive one and it is BY THIS PROJECT'S OWN SEATS:**\n\n    default:/*  #196: (Codex/Fable) d->color_mode is guest-controlled; an\n                unimplemented WID mode must not exit() the host (get_rgb is\n                per-pixel, so don't fatal()-flood either) -- render black.  */\n            *r = *g = *b = 0;\n\n**That is the same question already answered**: a GUEST-CONTROLLED unimplemented colour mode, in a PER-PIXEL\nhelper, on a SIBLING SGI framebuffer -- and the ruling was DEFINED ZEROS, with the per-pixel property given\nexplicitly as the reason not to `fatal()'-flood.  It is the identical reasoning `sgirandompixel' used for its\nlatch, so the house doctrine is consistent across three rounds.\n\n**SO THE DISPOSITION IS SETTLED BY TRANSFERRED WARRANT RATHER THAN BY PREFERENCE:** full-object zero, with a\ncomment saying plainly that hardware padding is unknown and this is defined behaviour rather than a CRIME\nmodel.  Both file-reading seats reached it independently, and `#196' is a fourth vote from a round that\nalready shipped.\n\n*This is the option-set rule working exactly as written -- `GREP THE FORK FOR AN EXISTING IDIOM; the tree's\nown precedents are part of the design space'.  I did not do that; grok did, and it produced a better answer\nthan the argument I was assembling.*\n\n*** FIFTH UNDER-HARVESTED ANSWER -- THE PACKET-FED SEATS.  glm's contains a DEFECT NOBODY ELSE FOUND\n(filed as [[sgilinearnegidx]]) and a BETTER VERSION OF MY OWN #95 ARGUMENT. ***\n\n**glm's `#95' warrant is stronger than the one I wrote and does not depend on reading the source:**\n*\"zeroing past `len' would itself be an OOB write on any caller with a tight buffer ... So every\nMEMORY-SAFE implementation of `#95' zeroes at most `bufdepth' bytes, and PATH B's `buf[bufdepth..3]' are\nstale under all of them, not merely the described one.  PATH A is a `MEM_WRITE' whose source is `buf'; a\nwrite-side pre-zero cannot exist there without destroying the payload of every device write in the tree.\nSurvival against `#95' is OVER-DETERMINED, not lucky.\"*  My version rested on the pre-zero happening to be\ngated on `MEM_READ'; glm's shows no memory-safe implementation could behave otherwise.\n\n**And a correction to my own §3b:** I wrote that `#95' zeroes `buf[0..bufdepth-1]' unconditionally.  glm:\n*\"that is the device branch only -- the stronger case presented as fact, harmless to the claim.\"*\n\n**minimax's witness-table point, recorded with its own arithmetic slip corrected:** it observes that the\nwitness table lists only RGBA mode words and omits the CI cases, which is fair -- CI leaks at any depth above\n1 and no row drives it.  Its example value is wrong, though: it calls `0x1210' *LIN_A | depth 2 | CI*, and\n`0x1210 & 0xf0' is `0x10' = RGB, not CI (`0x00').  **The observation stands and the constant does not.**\n\n*** SHARPENED 2026-09-07 BY THE glm SEAT on the `sgirergbroundtrip` pass-1 panel (_scratchpad/panel_20260907_051055), and it is\nwider than this row said. ***\n\n    \"per the code's own #100 comment a bufdepth of 8 makes every named PUT case transmit four\n     uninitialized stack bytes to guest memory; a bufdepth-8 GET silently drops the high half;\n     and with bufdepth 1 or 2 the RGB/RGBA/ABGR GETs read uninitialized buf[2..3].\"\n\n**THREE DISTINCT EXPOSURES, not one**, and the row had only the first:\n  (a) bufdepth 8 PUT -- `buf[4..7]` are never written by ANY arm, so four uninitialised stack bytes\n      go to guest memory through `memory_rw(..., MEM_WRITE)`;\n  (b) bufdepth 8 GET -- the high half is read into buf and then silently discarded, since every GET\n      arm reads at most four bytes.  Not a leak; a correctness gap;\n  (c) **bufdepth 1 or 2 GET -- the RGB, RGBA and ABGR arms read `buf[2..3]` that `memory_rw` never\n      filled.**  This is the one the row did not have, and it is the mirror of (a): a LEAK INTO THE\n      GUEST'S OWN PIXEL VALUE rather than into guest memory.\n\n**THE GUARD/BODY SPLIT IS WHY ALL THREE EXIST:** the colour type is bits 4-7 of the mode word and\n`bufdepth` is bits 8-9 of the SAME word, read independently.  Nothing couples them, so a guest\nselects a four-byte colour type with a one-byte transfer length whenever it likes.\n\n*Recorded from `#505`'s pre-table GUARD-FIELD-vs-BODY-FIELD check, which found the same disagreeing\nstate independently and then DECLINED to row it -- the exposure is identical before and after that\nround's edit (the shipped GET read three bytes, the fixed one reads three bytes), so a row there\nwould attribute a pre-existing defect to a round that neither created nor cured it.*\n\n*** PASS-1 DESIGN AUDIT, 2026-09-07 -- BY grok, BECAUSE CODEX REFUSED THE BRIEF.\n`regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/grok_pass1_20260907.txt`, 18 kB. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"GO-WITH-CHANGES.  Zero-fill `buf` at its current declaration is the right fix for\nTHIS class and is the whole fix OF this class.  It is not the packing fix, it is not PUT-only, and\nno single-access guest value of 0 can pin it.  S1 and S5 hold.  S2's decode holds.  S3's GET\noverstates RGB.  S4's quotes exist and its site-count does not.\"*\n\n**Q1/Q2 ANSWERED, and Q2's cost objection dissolved by a fact I did not have:** *\"It is declared\nAFTER the coordinate / `tlb_mode` / `tileptr`-valid early returns, so a zero-fill at that site is not\npaid on declined pixels.\"*  So the per-pixel cost applies only to pixels that were going to be\ntransferred anyway -- which removes the reason to prefer a narrower site.\n\n**Q3 ANSWERED, and it is the answer I asked for rather than the one I wanted:** *\"no single-access\nguest value of `0` can pin it.\"*  A value oracle cannot distinguish \"filled with zeros\" from\n\"happened to be zeros\", which is what the brief suspected and what the round must now design around.\n\n*** MY S3 WAS WRONG IN ITS EMPHASIS, AND grok's ENUMERATION IS BETTER THAN MY PROSE. ***  I led with\ndepth 8; the severe case is CI:\n\n    PUT bytes transferred with no store:\n                              depth 1   depth 2   depth 4     depth 8\n      CI                      none      buf[1]    buf[1..3]   buf[1..7]\n      RGB/RGBA/ABGR/default   none      none      none        buf[4..7]\n\n**CI at depth 4 already transfers three unstored bytes**, which needs no unusual depth at all -- and\nthe MTE path forces CI (see [[sgimtedepth4dead]]).  *\"emphasis on depth 8 is the weak vehicle.\"*\n\n**S2 REFINED, and the refinement matters for how this is described:** on the MTE path the colour bits\nare NOT a second guest field.  `src_mode`/`dst_mode` are built as `(tlb << 10) + (depth_field << 8)`\nwith no type bits, so `color_mode` is pinned at 0 (CI) unless the dead branch fires.  *\"That is a\nconstant colliding with a guest depth, not two independent guest fields.\"*  The two-independent-fields\nstory is true of the DE path and false of the MTE path.\n\n*** TWO NEW FINDINGS UNDER THE FOUND-SOMETHING-ELSE LICENCE: ***\n  1. **`DE_MODE_PIXDEPTH_*` (bits 2-3) is never read by `dev_sgi_re.c` at all.**  A third mode field\n     the device ignores entirely.\n  2. **A WRONG RECORD IN PRODUCT CODE, verified by reading:** `dev_sgi_re.c:732` says\n     `// bufdepth = 1, 2, or 4.` immediately above\n     `int src_bufdepth = 1 << ((src_mode >> 8) & 3);` -- which yields **1, 2, 4 or 8**.  The `#100`\n     round grew `buf[8]` precisely because the decode reaches 8, so this comment was already false\n     when that round shipped.  **L4, cheap, and it belongs to whichever round next touches this\n     file.**\n\n**S4's SITE COUNT IS WRONG AND ITS QUOTES ARE NOT.**  I wrote that `memory_rw.c` zero-fills at three\nsites; grok says the count does not hold and flagged in its own trace that *\"GET versus PUT may not\nshare a `#95` path\"*.  **That distinction is load-bearing and I had glossed it:** `#95` zero-fills a\nREAD buffer for `len` bytes on a FAILED access.  It therefore does nothing for a PUT (a write), and\non a successful GET it fills exactly `bufdepth` bytes -- never `buf[2..3]` beyond them.  **So the\ntransferred warrant is weaker than the brief claimed: `#95` establishes the fork's ETHOS for this\nclass, not a mechanism that already covers these calls.**\n\n*Recorded as the round's own premise-audit result: of five settled premises, two survived intact, one\nwas refined, one had its emphasis corrected, and one had its supporting count refuted.*\n\n*** A SECOND, INDEPENDENT AUDIT AGREES ON THE FIX AND GOES FURTHER ON THE ORACLE.  codex, via the\nREWORDED brief (`codex_reworded_20260907.txt`) after the original was refused. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"Zero-initialize all eight bytes at the existing declaration.  That completes the local\ninitialization repair in both directions, but does not establish correct pixel-format or MTE\nbehavior.  **No guest-visible value or write-count oracle can reliably prove initialization\noccurred.**\"*\n\n**TWO SEATS, TWO TRANSPORTS, SAME FIX.**  grok: *\"Zero-fill `buf` at its current declaration is the\nright fix for THIS class and is the whole fix OF this class.\"*  codex: *\"Zero-initialize all eight\nbytes at the existing declaration.\"*  Both add the same caveat -- it repairs initialisation and does\nNOT repair the packing/format defect, which is [[sgimtedepth4dead]]'s territory.  **The design is\nsettled.**\n\n*** THE ORACLE IS HARDER THAN THE NEIGHBOURING ROUND ASSUMED, AND THIS IS THE LOAD-BEARING FINDING.\n***  grok said no single-access guest VALUE can pin it.  codex rules out the WRITE-COUNT oracle as\nwell -- and a write-count oracle is exactly what a seat proposed for [[sgirandomcolour]]\n(*\"observe ZERO MEM_WRITE calls from an unsupported PUT\"*).  **That proposal survives for\n`sgirandomcolour`, where the question is whether a write HAPPENS; it does not transfer to this row,\nwhere the question is what the written bytes CONTAIN.**  Two rounds, one idiom, and it is valid in\nonly one of them.\n\n**CONSEQUENCE FOR THE ROUND'S SHAPE:** if no black-box guest-visible oracle can prove\ninitialisation, this round's detector cannot be a rung-3 probe of the ordinary kind.  The candidates\nare an offline differential over the arms, or a `harness:` witness under the ladder's fifth term.\n**That must be decided BEFORE the fix, not after** -- the round is otherwise a one-line edit with no\nway to defend it.\n\n**AND THE CEILING FOR THIS ROW IS NOW STATED CLEANLY** (same answer, Q1): *\"initializing the buffer\nchanges neither the transfer length nor the call count.  Uninitialized bytes can happen to match the\nexpected initialized values, so even matching values PLUS matching counts cannot establish\ninitialization.  **Unexpected values can expose a failure; expected values cannot prove\ndefinedness.**\"*\n\nThat is the sharpest form of the limit and it settles the round's shape: **no guest-visible oracle\ncan prove this fix worked.**  The detector must be an offline differential or a `harness:` witness\nunder the ladder's fifth term -- decided BEFORE the one-line edit, not after.\n\n*** FULL PANEL 2026-09-07 (`_scratchpad/panel_20260907_130159`).  SIX SEATS, ZERO DOWN, glm did\nNOT burn for the second panel running. ***\n\n**THE SPLIT, which is the thing for the adjudication:**\n  * **grok: TAKE THE ROUND** -- *\"zero-fill `buf` at the existing declaration; do not make the arms\n    write `bufdepth` bytes; do not treat a guest 0 as proof of initialisation.\"*\n  * **deepseek: NOT RATIFY as a correctness fix** -- *\"zero-fill only converts uninitialized reads\n    into defined-but-wrong bytes for CI depth>1; the independent assignment/transfer defect\n    remains.\"*\n  * **minimax: the symptom is handled but THE LICENCE IS UNSUPPORTED** -- *\"no measured delta is\n    offered.\"*\n\n  *So the question is not whether to zero-fill.  It is whether zero-fill is a FIX or a SYMPTOM PATCH\n  that leaves the real decoupling defect in place -- and whether the addition licence permits it with\n  no measured delta.*\n\n*** THREE SEATS INDEPENDENTLY FOUND A DEFECT IN MY PACKET, AND IT IS A CROSS-ROUND INTERACTION I HAD\nNOT SEEN. ***  S3's REVERSE-DIRECTION sentence -- *\"at depth 1 or 2 the read fills only that many\nbytes and the multi-byte arms then read `buf[2..3]` regardless\"* -- is **false of RGB as the file now\nsits**.  grok: *\"its GET sentence is false of RGB.\"*  glm: *\"asserted in prose, not in the enumerated\ntable, and under the packet's own model it is wrong.\"*  minimax: *\"S3's table is asymmetric -- write\ndirection enumerated, read direction mentioned in one sentence only.\"*\n\n  **WHY: `#505` changed RGB GET from `buf[1..3]` to `buf[0..2]`.**  So post-`#505` an RGB read at\n  depth 2 touches `buf[2]` and NOT `buf[3]`.  The sentence was true of the pre-`#505` file and I\n  carried it forward into a packet written after the fix.  **My own two rounds interact and the\n  enumerated table hid it, because I enumerated the WRITE direction and prosed the READ direction.**\n  *A table beside a sentence invites exactly this: the table gets checked and the sentence does not.*\n\n**THREE SEATS ALSO AGREE S4's WARRANT IS DEAD:** *\"S4 is ethos, not precedent\"* (deepseek, minimax);\n*\"its site-count and the `#95` transfer do not [hold]\"* (grok).  I had already recorded `#95` as\nweaker than claimed; the panel makes it unanimous.  **The addition licence therefore wants a MEASURED\nDELTA for this one-line change, and none exists.**  That is now the round's blocking question rather\nthan the oracle.\n\n**Q1 IS SETTLED AGAINST DEPTH-AWARE ARMS**, by two seats and for the same reason: *\"depth-aware arms\nwould trespass on the neighbouring rows that own pixel semantics and would require hardware truth\nthis packet does not contain\"* (kimi).  So the boundary with [[sgimtedepth4dead]] and\n[[sgirandomcolour]] holds.\n\n*** AND THE ORACLE QUESTION HAS ITS FIRST CONSTRUCTIVE ANSWER, from the kimi substitute: ***\n*\"The only honest definedness detector is an instrumented run (MSan/Valgrind class); any harness\nwitness is one-sided **UNLESS THE STACK BENEATH THE FRAME IS PRE-SEEDED**.\"*\n\n  **That is actionable and nobody had proposed it.**  Every earlier answer said no oracle can prove\n  definedness; this says a harness witness becomes TWO-SIDED if the frame's stack is seeded with a\n  known non-zero pattern first -- then `0` means initialised and the pattern means not.  This fork\n  already has an ASAN/UBSan tree (`build-asan/`), so the instrumented route is also available.\n  **Both need checking before the round; neither was on the table an hour ago.**\n\n*** THE CODEX SEAT REFUTED S4 AND THE OTHER THREE SEATS RATIFIED MY ERROR.  SETTLED BY OPENING\nTHE FILE, NOT BY COUNTING SEATS. ***\n\nMy S4 said `#95` *\"fills a READ buffer for `len` bytes on a FAILED access\"*.  deepseek, minimax and\ngrok all agreed and graded the warrant *\"ethos, not precedent\"*.  **codex went and read it:**\n\n    memory_rw.c :492   if (writeflag == MEM_READ) memset(data, 0, len);\n                :494   paddr -= mem->devices[i].baseaddr;      <- THE DEVICE DISPATCH\n\n**`:492` is not in a failure branch at all.**  It prefills on EVERY device read, successful or not,\nbefore dispatch and before length clamping -- its own comment says *\"never hand back uninitialised\nhost memory to the guest.  Root-cause fix for the uninit-read class\"*.  The failure-path fills are\n`:215` and `:267`, the two that `return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED`.  **Verified here by reading all six\nsites.**  codex's enumeration of six (215, 267, 492, 671, 683, 732) is correct and my 'three sites'\nwas wrong as well.\n\n  > **SO THE WARRANT IS A PRECEDENT, NOT AN ETHOS: `#95` is exactly 'initialise storage before a\n  > producer may assign only part of it'.**  That is this fix, one layer down.  The addition licence\n  > is satisfied by TRANSFERRED WARRANT and the round does NOT owe a measured delta -- which\n  > REVERSES the blocking question three seats had just created.\n\n**THIS IS THE WRONG-FACT-COMES-BACK-CONFIRMED CLASS AT FULL STRENGTH.**  I stated a weakened S4 as a\nSETTLED PREMISE; three of six seats confirmed it; one seat checked it.  The carrier's rule --\n*unanimity on a brief-supplied fact is worth nothing; score agreement only where a seat re-derived\nit* -- scores this exactly right, and I had written the premise-audit opener into the brief and\nstill stated S4 as settled rather than as a question.\n\n*The sharper lesson: **a premise I had ALREADY CORRECTED ONCE, in the weakening direction, is the\nmost dangerous kind.**  It arrives wearing the clothes of a correction, so it reads as the careful\nversion and nobody re-checks it -- including me, who wrote it.*\n\n**codex's other corrections, each verified before recording:**\n  * **S1 is wrong.**  Not 'once per pixel from three callers': THREE execution paths, TWO caller\n    functions, SEVEN direct call sites (LINE GET :817 PUT :863; RECTANGLE GET :878, GET :883,\n    PUT :929; tiled MTE GET :1382 PUT :1387).  RECTANGLE does TWO GETs and a PUT per iteration, so\n    'once per pixel' understates a fill's cost threefold on that path.\n  * **S3's GET row corrected, agreeing with grok, glm and minimax** -- per-arm and per-width: CI\n    consumes NO unassigned byte at any width; RGB consumes `buf[1..2]` after a 1-byte read and\n    `buf[2]` after 2; RGBA/ABGR/default consume `buf[1..3]` and `buf[2..3]`.  **`#505` is why RGB\n    differs** -- it now reads `buf[0..2]` and never `buf[3]`.\n  * *** `-DNDEBUG` IS SET, so an `assert()`-based detector is a NO-OP in a normal build. ***\n    Verified: `configure:313` sets `CFLAGS=\"-DNDEBUG $CFLAGS\"`, and `src/devices/Makefile:9` carries\n    it.  An initialisedness harness built on `assert` would be VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION -- the\n    check-that-cannot-fail shape, arriving inside the detector for this very round.\n  * **Q4, a coverage gap in the SHIPPED `#505` detector:** `rgb depth64` and `rgb tiled64` widen the\n    *source read*; their destination stays a four-byte RGBA write, so **no row establishes\n    initialisation of an eight-byte PUT's tail.**  Filed as [[sgireput8tail]] rather than fixed.",
GXEMUL-SEC\tools\pipeline\ledger.json:30496:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07.  The lane-semantics half was raised by the codex seat (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt) with an\nenumerated table; the third defect I found by reading the function while checking that table.\n\n    ofs = relative_addr & 3;                       <- guest address bits 5-6\n    if (ofs > len) { fatal(...); exit(1); }        <- `>' where `>=' was meant\n    ...\n    uint8_t byte;                                  <- never initialised\n    cpu->memory_rw(..., &byte, 1, MEM_READ, ...);  <- result DISCARDED\n    for (i = 0; i < len; i++)\n            data[i] = i == ofs ? byte : 0x00;\n\n**1. OFF-BY-ONE.**  The guard is `>' rather than `>=', so ofs == len passes.  codex's table, re-derived:\n\n    len=1  ofs {0,1} accepted      len=4  ofs {0,1,2,3} accepted\n    len=2  ofs {0,1,2} accepted    len=8  ofs {0,1,2,3} accepted\n\nFor (len,ofs) = (1,1) and (2,2) the loop never reaches i == ofs, so **the access performs a real downstream\nread and returns ALL ZEROS**.  The guest is told the device answered zero when that lane was never asked.\n\n**2. THE READ PATH DISCARDS THE FAILURE RESULT** that the WRITE path returns -- codex's observation, and the\nasymmetry is visible with the two branches side by side.\n\n**3. `byte' IS UNINITIALISED and is consumed if that discarded read failed.**  This is the FOURTH instance\nof the uninitialised-consumption family recorded on 2026-09-07, alongside [[sgirebufuninit]],\n[[sgigbeconvover]] and #95's incomplete class closure.  **#95 zeroes device READ buffers, so a\ndevice-served read is covered; a read that FAILS before reaching a handler is not** -- the same gap\nsgirebufuninit turns on.\n\nMED rather than HIGH: the wrong-lane read is a correctness defect with no host death and nothing disclosed\nbut a stale stack byte.  The exit(1) in the same function is [[lcabadval]]'s range site, graded there.\n\nNO WITNESS.  Reachability of the (1,1)/(2,2) pairs is established by reading; nothing shows a guest driving\nthem.",
GXEMUL-SEC\tools\pipeline\ledger.json:30506:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07, raised by the codex seat (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt) and verified:\n\n    for (dev = 0; dev < 21; dev++)\n            if (tag & (0x800 << dev))\n                    break;\n\n0x800 is a plain int (2^11).  At dev == 20 the expression is 2^31, which overflows a signed 32-bit int --\n**undefined behaviour** in C99, not merely implementation-defined.\n\n**The irony worth recording: this loop is the file's own PRECEDENT for graceful degradation.**  Its\n`dev >= 21' fallback has a commented-out fatal()/exit(1) and executes `dev = 0;' instead -- the\ndelete-the-check-and-substitute-a-safe-default disposition that [[lcabadval]] cites as a transferable\nin-tree warrant.  **The safe path contains UB.**\n\nLOW: `0x800U << dev' fixes it, and on every compiler this project targets the emitted code is already what\nwas intended.  Recorded because a UB shift is exactly what a sanitiser build exists to catch, and\nbuild-asan/ is kept clean -- **so the first question is whether a UBSan sweep already flags it, and if not,\nwhy not.**",
GXEMUL-SEC\tools\pipeline\ledger.json:30516:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07, raised by the codex seat (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt) while scoping an unrelated device, and\nverified at both ends.\n\n    memory_alpha.c   (*return_paddr) = ALPHA_PTE_TO_PFN(pte3) << pageSizeShift;\n    memory_rw.c      offset = paddr & offset_mask;\n\nThe walk returns a PAGE-BASE address, so `paddr & offset_mask' is ZERO and every access within a page\nresolves to that page's first bytes.\n\n*** THE HEDGE IS THE SEAT'S OWN AND IT IS CORRECT: the DIRECT-MAPPED paths avoid it. ***  Both early returns\n-- K0SEG (`vaddr & 0x000003ffffffffffULL') and the `ptbr == 0' bootstrap hack -- MASK the vaddr and therefore\nKEEP the offset bits.  Only the real walk drops them, and it is reached in user mode, or outside K0SEG once\nptbr != 0.\n\n**REACHABILITY IS THE OPEN QUESTION AND IT BOUNDS THE SEVERITY BOTH WAYS.**  memory_alpha.c carries seven\nTODOs and an explicit fatal(\"Alpha kernel virtual memory seems to want to be used. Not yet implemented\") +\nexit(1), so Alpha VM here is immature and a bootable configuration may never reach the walk -- in which case\nthis is latent rather than live.  **HIGH is PROVISIONAL on that**: if a guest reaches it the effect is total\nmemory-access corruption; if none can, the row is documented-only.  A round takes the reachability question\nFIRST; the fix itself is adding the offset back.\n\nThe function is live, not dead code: cpu_alpha.c assigns `cpu->translate_v2p = alpha_translate_v2p' and\ncpu_dyntrans.c calls it.\n\n*** REACHABILITY PASS, codex seat 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/alphapfnoffset/codex_reachability_20260907.txt).  THE ARITHMETIC IS CONFIRMED BY\nEXHAUSTIVE ENUMERATION -- AND MY OWN SUMMARY OF THE IMPACT WAS TOO BROAD.  Corrected here. ***\n\n**CONFIRMED, and by walking the values rather than arguing:** *\"I enumerated all 8,192 offsets: the walk\nexpression produces offset zero throughout, differing from the offset-preserving address for 8,191 offsets.\nThe masking paths preserve offsets; exhaustive enumeration also confirmed preservation throughout all three\nbootstrap alias regions.\"*  So the defect is real, the direct-mapped arms are genuinely safe, and there are\nTHREE bootstrap alias regions rather than the two I had read.\n\n*** MY OVERCLAIM, CORRECTED: this row said `every walked access would land at its page base'.  THAT IS TOO\nBROAD. ***  codex: *\"Slow-path accesses consume the incorrect physical offset, but cached Alpha loads/stores\ncalculate `addr & 8191' themselves.  Subsequent cache hits can therefore access the correct within-page\nbytes.\"*  So the corruption is confined to the SLOW PATH; once a page is cached, the load/store instructions\nrecompute the offset and read the right bytes.  **That materially narrows the blast radius** and it is the\nsecond time tonight this seat has narrowed one of my summaries -- the first being the class-scan denominator.\n\n**REACHABILITY -- the answer is NOT `latent', and it is not `live' either.**  codex found *\"no source barrier\nmaking a guest-instruction witness impossible on the existing description\"*, and named the smallest decisive\nobservation: one COMPLETED guest data access on the existing `eb164' machine that reaches the walk with a\nnonzero page offset and whose caller completes the access on the returned page-base address.  **A PTBR\nwatchpoint or a walker-entry breakpoint is explicitly NOT enough** -- reaching the walk is not the same as an\naccess completing wrongly.\n\n**What it could not settle, and said so plainly:** *\"I cannot name a demonstrated working Alpha OS boot from\nthe allowed files.\"*  So the honest status is **WITNESSABLE IN PRINCIPLE, UNWITNESSED IN FACT** -- which is\na different thing from latent, and it is what a round takes first.\n\n**HIGH stands but its warrant changes**: not `every access is corrupted' (false), but `the slow path is\ncorrupted for 8,191 of 8,192 offsets, on a path with no established guest that reaches it'.\n\n*** THE BOOT QUESTION IS ANSWERED, AND IT REVERSES MY CONCERN: ALL FOUR ALPHA ROWS ARE COLD-DEBUGGER\nREACHABLE.  NO OS BOOT IS REQUIRED.  codex, 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/alphapfnoffset/codex_boot_20260907.txt). ***\n\nI had flagged the risk that the Alpha queue was accumulating well-analysed rows on a machine nothing can\nexercise.  It is not: *\"EB164 or AlphaServer 4100 provides the required unmodified machine description.\nThere is no architectural requirement to boot an OS first.\"*  Debugger word writes go through the memory\nhelpers, Alpha fetches and decodes through its actual memory path, and the shared breakpoint machinery\napplies -- which is exactly the rung-3 cold-debugger shape this project's ladder admits.\n\nIts classification of all four:\n\n    page-walk drops the offset      COLD-DEBUGGER REACHABLE   needs page-table state; the bootstrap\n                                                              identity mapping bypasses the walk\n    PALcode register mismatch       COLD-DEBUGGER REACHABLE   swpctx is reached by guest dispatch\n    unsupported fault-delivery      COLD-DEBUGGER REACHABLE   failed access returns without an exception\n    guest-reachable process exit    COLD-DEBUGGER REACHABLE   the bootstrap translator's special branch\n\n**The qualification on THIS row is the one that matters for its witness:** the walk needs page-table state,\nand *the ordinary bootstrap identity mapping bypasses the walk*.  So a probe must first establish a non-zero\n`apcb_ptbr' and a valid three-level table -- which is real work, and is why this row is still the hardest of\nthe five despite being the most severe if reached.\n\n**[[alphaswpctxptbr]] is a confound for that witness and must be fixed first:** it clobbers `apcb_ptbr` with\nthe flags word, so any probe that sets up a context via `swpctx` and then observes a bad translation cannot\nsay which defect produced it.",
