15450: ## 2026-09-07 — four rows from ONE scoped codex invocation on `dev_lca.c`, plus the warrant `lcabadval` was missing
15451: 
15452: All four came from a single read-only codex run (`regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt`),
15453: the second run under the new §C5 discipline — neutral language, reading scoped to `src/` and `regress/*.py`.
15454: It completed at 297,783 bytes with **zero** content-filter flags, on a device unrelated to the one that
15455: produced the rule. **Every claim below was verified against the source before filing; one of the four I found
15456: by reading the function while checking codex's table, not from the answer itself.**
15457: 
15458: **`lcabadval` (HIGH, existing) — the severity warrant it was missing.**  Each window guard STORES the value
15459: and *then* ends the process:
15460: 
15461:     d->window_mask_0 = idata;                          <- the store LANDS
15462:     if (idata != 0x700000ULL) { fatal(...); exit(1); } <- then the process ends
15463: 
15464: And **zero is a documented encoding** — `alpha_lcareg.h` defines `IOC_W_MASK_1M` as `0x0`, the 1 MB window.
15465: So a guest selecting the smallest documented window size kills the emulator; likewise `W_BASE1`, whose one
15466: accepted constant carries the `WEN` *window enable* bit, so a driver **disabling** a window is killed too.
15467: That is `#504`'s warrant at a second device: documented values the model dies on, not junk ones.
15468: 
15469: **Its RANGE site is also far more reachable than the row said.**  It was recorded as `ofs > len`; in fact
15470: `relative_addr >>= 5; ofs = relative_addr & 3;` — **`ofs` is guest address bits 5-6**, so an ordinary 1-byte
15471: access at an address with those bits set to 2 or 3 ends the process.  That is address decoding, not a value
15472: whitelist, and it belongs to a different disposition class from the six value guards.
15473: 
15474: **`lcaisalane` (MED) — three defects in eleven lines.**  The guard is `>` where `>=` was meant, so
15475: `(len,ofs) = (1,1)` and `(2,2)` pass, perform a real downstream read, and **return all zeros** because no `i`
15476: ever equals `ofs` — the guest is told the device answered zero when that lane was never asked.  The read path
15477: **discards** the failure result the write path returns.  And `uint8_t byte;` is **uninitialised** and consumed
15478: if that discarded read failed — **the fourth instance of the uninitialised-consumption family today**,
15479: alongside `sgirebufuninit`, `sgigbeconvover` and `#95`'s incomplete class closure.
15480: 
15481: **`alphapfnoffset` (HIGH, provisional) — the page-table walk drops the within-page offset.**
15482: `(*return_paddr) = ALPHA_PTE_TO_PFN(pte3) << pageSizeShift` returns a page base, and `memory_rw` then computes
15483: `offset = paddr & offset_mask`, which is zero — so every walked access lands at its page's first bytes.
15484: **Codex's hedge is correct and I verified it:** both direct-mapped paths mask the vaddr and *keep* the offset,
15485: so only the real walk is affected.  **HIGH is provisional on reachability** — `memory_alpha.c` carries seven
15486: TODOs and an explicit "not yet implemented" exit, so a bootable config may never reach the walk, in which case
15487: this is latent.  A round settles reachability *first*; the fix is adding the offset back.
15488: 
15489: **`alphavmkill` (MED) — a guest virtual address ends the host.**  `vaddr == 0xfffffffd80000000` hits
15490: `fatal(...); exit(1);` inside the `ptbr == 0` bootstrap arm, so it is reached early in a boot on an address the
15491: kernel chooses.  **The disposition is genuinely not obvious**, which is why it is filed rather than fixed:
15492: declining leaves the guest with a wrong translation to fault on somewhere less legible, so this may be one of
15493: the rare sites where a loud stop beats continuing — and if so the round's product is a record saying so plus a
15494: latch, not a removal.
15495: 
15496: **`lcashiftub` (LOW) — `0x800 << dev` reaches `2^31` in a signed 32-bit `int`**, undefined behaviour.  The
15497: irony is worth the line: **that loop is the file's own precedent for graceful degradation** — its `dev >= 21`
15498: fallback has a commented-out `exit(1)` and substitutes a safe default, which `lcabadval` cites as a
15499: transferable in-tree warrant.  The safe path contains UB.  `0x800U` fixes it; the first question is whether a
15500: UBSan sweep already flags it, and if not, why not.
15501: 
15502: ## 2026-09-07 — CORRECTION to this morning's `alphapfnoffset` entry, plus two more Alpha rows
15503: 
15504: **MY OWN OVERCLAIM, CORRECTED.**  The entry above says of the Alpha page-table walk:
15505: 
15506: > `offset = paddr & offset_mask`, which is zero — so **every walked access lands at its page's first bytes.**
