14389: > *** THE SHARPEST INSTANCE, verified by computation and by reading the arm: `dev_pvr.c`'s
14390: > `if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE && idata != 0x0cff0000)` paired with `(int) idata`. *** A guest
14391: > writing `0x1_0cff0000` fails the 64-bit compare and the host dies — and the message prints
14392: > `0x0cff0000`, **exactly the value the code says is acceptable**. The diagnostic tells a
14393: > maintainer the guest wrote the correct value and died for it.
14394: >
14395: > **Severity, reasoned rather than assumed:** none of this changes *whether* the host dies — the
14396: > guards are unchanged. It changes what the log says about *why*, at the one moment the log is all
14397: > that survives. A records-correctness defect in product code, not a control-flow one.
14398: >
14399: > **NOT a one-line sweep.** Which sites are genuinely reachable with a >32-bit datum depends on
14400: > register width and the guest paths that reach them, and a blanket edit to 45 diagnostics is the
14401: > unlicensed addition this project refuses. The honest first round is the ~20 full-width-guard
14402: > sites, ordered by whether a guest can deliver a >32-bit write at all.
14403: 
14404: ### `mtetlbflood` (low) -- filed 2026-08-31: an unlatched guest-drivable `fatal()` in the MTE
14405: 
14406: `dev_sgi_re.c`'s MTE `switch (dst_tlb)` implements TLB A/B/C and LINEAR_A/LINEAR_B. `dst_tlb` is
14407: a **3-bit guest-controlled field** — `(mode & 0x1c) >> 2` — so values 3 (`MTE_TLB_TEX`), 6
14408: (`MTE_TLB_CLIP`) and 7 reach `default:`.
14409: 
14410: That arm is a bare `fatal()` with **no `exit(1)`**, so it is *not* a host kill — but it carries
14411: **no latch either**, so a guest looping over MTE operations floods host stdout without bound.
14412: `floodclass` family. The house idiom is `N = 1` per device instance with the suppression marker
