651: #  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
652: #  A CLASS RATCHET, not a bug fix.  Task from the R6 pass-2 kimi seat, whose
653: #  answer had gone unread for a day: "sweep for other divisions in
654: #  debug()/fatal() argument position with guest-writable operands -- ns16550 is
655: #  unlikely to be the only instance of that shape."
656: #
657: #  The shape is worth a ratchet because C evaluates an argument whether or not
658: #  the callee prints it, so a division there runs at EVERY verbosity.  A grep
659: #  cannot find it: the operator has to be in argument position with literals and
660: #  comments already stripped, and the call may span lines.  diag_div_sweep.py
661: #  balances the parens and does that; it also splits constant divisors (which
662: #  cannot be zero, so are not the shape) from drivable ones.
663: #
664: #  MEASURED 2026-08-16: 24 sites, 22 constant, 2 non-constant -- so kimi's
665: #  hypothesis is REFUTED, and the ratchet is what keeps the refutation true.
666: #    * dev_ns16550.c, anchor `115200 / d->divisor` -- the live one, filed `ns16550`.
667: #      The other 115200 in that file is `115200 / 9600`, a CONSTANT divisor and not
668: #      the shape, so anchor on the `d->divisor` form rather than on the number.
669: #    * diskimage.c, anchor `(int) (bytes_left % ct->sector_size)` -- NOT drivable:
670: #      every unsupported TRACK mode reaches a `goto fail` immediately following the
671: #      diagnostic anchored at `unsupported TRACK mode`, and `nr_of_pt ++` runs only
672: #      after that, so a counted track always carries 2048/2336/2352.  The plain
673: #      division -- anchor `nr_sectors = bytes_left / ct->sector_size` -- would fault
674: #      first regardless.
675: #
676: #  *** THESE WERE LINE NUMBERS UNTIL 2026-08-30 AND THREE OF THE FIVE HAD DRIFTED. ***
677: #  `dev_ns16550.c:79` had become a comment line (the division is at :181);
678: #  `diskimage.c:543` was wrong by 440 lines (:983); and `:819` named the LAST ARGUMENT
679: #  LINE of the diagnostic rather than the `goto fail` below it -- the
680: #  read-the-line-before-citing-it trap, inside a citation whose only job is to be
681: #  re-checked.  `:823` and `:974` were still correct, so this is drift and not a bad
682: #  transcription.  Every anchor above was verified to match EXACTLY ONCE when written;
683: #  the bare form `bytes_left % ct->sector_size` matches TWICE and is deliberately not
684: #  used.  An anchor that stops matching is LOUD; a drifted number is silent.
685: #  A THIRD site means someone added a new instance of the class: read it before
686: #  raising this number.
687: DIVSWEEP=$HERE/diag_div_sweep.py
688: if [ -f "$DIVSWEEP" ] && command -v python3 > /dev/null 2>&1; then
689:     dsout=$(python3 "$DIVSWEEP" 2>/dev/null)
690:     check "class ratchet: drivable divisions in diagnostic argument position" \
691:           "$(printf '%s\n' "$dsout" | grep -oE 'DIAG_DIV_NONCONST=[0-9]+' | cut -d= -f2)" "2"
692:     #  The denominator too: if the sweep silently stopped finding ANYTHING the
693:     #  count above would still read 2 only by luck, and a zero total is the
694:     #  classic vacuous green.
695:     #  *** 24 -> 21 ON 2026-08-29, AND IT IS A CONSOLIDATION, NOT THREE REMOVALS. ***
696:     #  NOT ONE INSTANCE OF THE CLASS LEFT THE CODE.  `7c9dd9e` (#462) merged FOUR
697:     #  one-division fatal() calls -- grep anchor `dma_reg[4 / sizeof(uint32_t)]` --
698:     #  into ONE fatal() carrying the SAME FOUR DIVISIONS as its arguments.
699:     #  DIAG_DIV_TOTAL counts CALLS carrying at least one division, so merging four
700:     #  into one moves the total while the division census is unchanged.  A round
701:     #  acting on the first reading would have hunted three removals that never were.
702:     #
703:     #  Attribution settled BY MEASUREMENT, because two seats disagreed (#490 vs #462):
704:     #      git show 7c9dd9e --format= -- <file> | grep -cE '^[+-].*dma_reg'  ->  9
705:     #      (the BARE `| grep -c dma_reg` form yields 11 -- it counts context
706:     #      lines too.  9 is the CHANGED-LINES count and is the right figure;
707:     #      the command printed here was the wrong one.  Caught by fable5.)
708:     #      git show fb0cc78 --format= -- <file> | grep -cE '^[+-].*dma_reg'  ->  0
709:     #
710:     #  *** CITED BY COMMIT AND ANCHOR, NOT BY LINE NUMBER, AND DELIBERATELY. ***  This
711:     #  sweep computes its printed line numbers from STRIPPED-text offsets indexed
712:     #  against the RAW file, so they run systematically small -- it reports the gdrom
713:     #  sites 400+ lines early, landing inside a comment block.  Its COUNTS do not
714:     #  depend on that and are sound.  Filed as `divsweeplines`; the remedy (a
715:     #  length-preserving strip) is measured to move the citations to their true
716:     #  call-start lines with the counts unchanged.  Do not paste its line numbers.
717:     #
718:     #  THE DOWN DIRECTION NEEDED ITS OWN INSTRUCTION AND NOW HAS ONE.  The rule above
719:     #  ("A THIRD site means someone added a new instance") is written for GROWTH.  A
720:     #  DECREASE is ambiguous: it can mean the code improved, or that the sweep quietly
721:     #  stopped finding things -- the vacuous-green shape this denominator exists to
722:     #  catch.  THE DISCRIMINATOR IS THE NUMERATOR: if DIAG_DIV_NONCONST still reads 2
723:     #  and names the same two sites, the sweep works and a lower total is real.  If
724:     #  the numerator moves too, suspect the TOOL before the code.  Then diff the site
725:     #  list against the last green tree AT FILE GRANULARITY, the lines being wrong.
726:     #
727:     #  FILED, NOT TAKEN HERE: a seat argued this should become a FLOOR, since an
728:     #  equality breaks on every legitimate de-fatalisation and de-fatalisation is this
729:     #  fork's whole queue.  The argument is good.  Converting it changes what the
730:     #  check PROVES, so it is not folded into a re-pin.
731:     check "class ratchet: the sweep still sees the whole call set" \
732:           "$(printf '%s\n' "$dsout" | grep -oE 'DIAG_DIV_TOTAL=[0-9]+' | cut -d= -f2)" "21"
733: else
734:     note "diag_div_sweep.py or python3 missing -- class ratchet NOT run"
735: fi
